1
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:57,700
It is an extremely common mistake,

2
00:01:57,910 --> 00:02:01,080
people think the writer's imagination is always at work,

3
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,280
that he's constantly inventing an endless supply...

4
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:06,080
of incidents and episodes,

5
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:09,960
that he simply dreams up his stories out of thin air.

6
00:02:10,170 --> 00:02:12,840
In point of fact, the opposite is true.

7
00:02:13,050 --> 00:02:15,010
Once the public knows you're a writer,

8
00:02:15,210 --> 00:02:17,880
they bring the characters and events to you...

9
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:20,760
and as long as you maintain your ability to look...

10
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:23,310
and to carefully listen, (DOOR OPENS)

11
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:26,100
these stories will continue to...

12
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:28,900
Stop it. Stop it! Don't! Don't do it! (TOY GUN CLICKS)

13
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:30,730
(PELLET CLATTERS) (FOOTSTEPS RECEDE)

14
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:38,570
Uh, will continue to seek you out over your lifetime.

15
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:42,120
To him who has often told the tales of others,

16
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:43,950
many tales will be told.

17
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:45,910
- Sorry.
- It's all right.

18
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:49,170
The incidents that follow were described to me

19
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:52,380
exactly as I present them here

20
00:02:52,590 --> 00:02:54,960
and in a wholly unexpected way.

21
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:03,760
<i>A number of years ago,</i>

22
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:06,850
<i>while suffering from a mild case of "Scribe's Fever,"</i>

23
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:08,850
<i>a form of neurasthenia common among...</i>

24
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:10,770
<i>the intelligentsia of that time,</i>

25
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:12,860
<i>I decided to spend the month of August...</i>

26
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,990
<i>in the spa town of Nebelsbad below the Alpine Sudetenwaltz,</i>

27
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:20,530
<i>and had taken up rooms in the Grand Budapest,</i>

28
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:25,580
<i>a picturesque, elaborate, and once widely celebrated establishment.</i>

29
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:28,330
<i>I expect some of you will know it.</i>

30
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:33,330
YOUNG WRITER: <i>It was off season and, by that time, decidedly out of fashion,</i>

31
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:35,460
<i>and it had already begun its descent...</i>

32
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:37,660
<i>into shabbiness and eventual demolition.</i>

33
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:41,590
<i>What few guests we were...</i>

34
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,140
<i>had quickly come to recognize one another by sight,,,</i>

35
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:47,390
<i>as the only living souls residing in the vast establishment,</i>

36
00:03:47,470 --> 00:03:48,560
(COINS CLINKING)

37
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,640
<i>although I do not believe any acquaintance among our number had proceeded...</i>

38
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:54,730
<i>beyond the polite nods we exchanged as we passed...</i>

39
00:03:54,810 --> 00:03:55,820
<i>in the Palm Court,</i>

40
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:57,860
<i>in the Arabian baths,</i>

41
00:03:58,070 --> 00:04:00,240
<i>and onboard the Colonnade Funicular.</i>

42
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:03,320
<i>We were a very reserved group, it seemed,</i>

43
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:06,560
<i>and, without exception, solitary.</i>

44
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:13,120
<i>Perhaps as a result of this general silence,</i>

45
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:16,130
<i>I had established a casual and bantering familiarity...</i>

46
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:18,340
<i>with the hotel's concierge, a West-continental...</i>

47
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:20,550
<i>known only as</i> Monsieur <i>Jean,</i>

48
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:22,590
<i>who struck one as being, at once,</i>

49
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,930
<i>both lazy and, really, quite accommodating.</i>

50
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:28,310
<i>I expect he was not well paid.</i>

51
00:04:28,510 --> 00:04:29,930
<i>In any case, one evening,</i>

52
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,600
<i>as I stood conferring elbow-to-elbow with</i> Monsieur <i>Jean,</i>

53
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:37,150
<i>as had become my habit, I noticed a new presence in our company.</i>

54
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:39,730
<i>A small, elderly man, smartly dressed,</i>

55
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,860
<i>with an exceptionally lively, intelligent face...</i>

56
00:04:43,070 --> 00:04:46,070
<i>and an immediately perceptible air of sadness.</i>

57
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:49,660
<i>He was, like the rest of us, alone, but also, I must say,</i>

58
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:54,420
<i>he was the first that struck one as being deeply and truly lonely.</i>

59
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:57,290
<i>A symptom of my own medical condition as well.</i>

60
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:59,380
Who's this interesting old fellow?

61
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:01,170
<i>I inquired of</i> Monsieur <i>Jean.</i>

62
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:04,010
<i>To my surprise, he was distinctly taken aback.</i>

63
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:05,470
Don't you know? <i>He asked.</i>

64
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:07,430
Don't you recognize him?

65
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:09,010
<i>He did look familiar.</i>

66
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:11,310
That's Mr. Moustafa himself.

67
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:12,930
He arrived earlier this morning.

68
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:15,350
<i>This name will no doubt be familiar...</i>

69
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:18,230
<i>to the more seasoned persons among you.</i>

70
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:24,900
<i>Mr. Zero Moustafa was at one time the richest man in Zubrowka,</i>

71
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:30,240
<i>and was still indeed the owner of the Grand Budapest.</i>

72
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:33,410
He often comes and stays a week or more,

73
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:35,750
three times a year at least, but never in the season.

74
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,290
Monsieur <i>Jean signaled to me and I leaned closer.</i>

75
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:39,540
I'll tell you a secret.

76
00:05:39,750 --> 00:05:42,090
He takes only a single-bed sleeping room without a bath...

77
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:44,050
in the rear corner of the top floor...

78
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,180
and it's smaller than the service elevator!

79
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:47,720
<i>It was well known,</i>

80
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:50,470
<i>Zero Moustafa had purchased and famously inhabited...</i>

81
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:53,600
<i>some of the most lavish castles and palazzos on the continent.</i>

82
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:57,100
<i>Yet here, in his own nearly empty hotel,</i>

83
00:05:57,310 --> 00:05:59,810
<i>he occupied a servant's quarters?</i>

84
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:01,690
<i>At that moment, the curtain rose...</i>

85
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,280
<i>on a parenthetical, domestic drama...</i>

86
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:04,650
Shit.

87
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:07,740
<i>...which required the immediate and complete attention...</i>

88
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:09,910
<i>of</i> Monsieur <i>Jean,</i> (DOG BARKING)

89
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,120
<i>but, frankly, did not hold mine for long.</i>

90
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:15,620
(ELEVATOR BELL DINGS)

91
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:22,420
<i>However,</i>

92
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:25,260
<i>this premature intermission in the story of the curious, old man</i>

93
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:27,880
<i>had left me, as the expression goes,</i>

94
00:06:28,090 --> 00:06:30,220
"gespannt wie ein Flitzebogen,"

95
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:32,720
<i>that is, on the edge of my seat,</i>

96
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:35,810
<i>where I remained throughout the next morning, until,</i>

97
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:39,770
<i>in what I have found to be its mysterious and utterly reliable fashion,</i>

98
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:43,860
<i>fate, once again, intervened on my behalf.</i>

99
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:49,780
MR. MOUSTAFA: I admire your work.

100
00:06:52,950 --> 00:06:54,410
I beg your pardon?

101
00:06:56,160 --> 00:07:00,290
I said, I know and admire your wonderful work.

102
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:03,090
Thank you most kindly, sir.

103
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:05,550
Did <i>Monsieur</i> Jean have a word or two to share with you...

104
00:07:05,630 --> 00:07:09,340
about the aged proprietor of this establishment?

105
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:14,060
I must confess, I did myself inquire about you.

106
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:16,850
He's perfectly capable, of course, <i>Monsieur</i> Jean

107
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:18,810
but we can't claim he's a first,

108
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:21,690
or, in earnest, even second-rate concierge.

109
00:07:21,770 --> 00:07:23,020
But there it is.

110
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,730
Times have changed.

111
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:31,950
The thermal baths are very beautiful.

112
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:33,700
They were in their first condition.

113
00:07:33,870 --> 00:07:35,450
It couldn't be maintained, of course.

114
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:37,370
Too decadent for current tastes.

115
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:42,250
But I love it all just the same, this enchanting old ruin.

116
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:45,880
How did you come to buy it, if I may ask?

117
00:07:46,090 --> 00:07:48,090
The Grand Budapest.

118
00:07:50,090 --> 00:07:51,970
(UNPLUGS DRAIN)

119
00:07:57,850 --> 00:07:58,850
I didn't.

120
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:00,810
(WATER RUNNING)

121
00:08:00,890 --> 00:08:02,440
(MAN YELLING)

122
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:07,780
(YELLING STOPS)

123
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:10,320
If you're not merely being polite,

124
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:13,240
and you must tell me if that's the case,

125
00:08:13,450 --> 00:08:16,450
but if it genuinely does interest you,

126
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:19,000
may I invite you to dine with me tonight,

127
00:08:19,290 --> 00:08:23,000
and it will be my pleasure and, indeed, my privilege to tell you

128
00:08:23,210 --> 00:08:26,630
"my story." Such as it is.

129
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,340
(ORDERING FOOD, INDISTINCT)

130
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:34,800
Two ducks roasted with olives.

131
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:37,390
- Rabbit, salad?
- Mmm.

132
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,310
Pouilly-Jouvet '52, plus a split of the brut.

133
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:44,650
That should provide us ample time

134
00:08:44,850 --> 00:08:45,940
if I commence promptly.

135
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,150
By all means.

136
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:05,580
(CHUCKLING)

137
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:12,510
Well, it begins, as it must, with our mutual friend's predecessor.

138
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:15,930
The beloved, original concierge of The Grand Budapest.

139
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:19,390
It begins, of course, with...

140
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:34,820
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

141
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:43,870
- Bring the table to the window.
- Yes, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

142
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:46,540
- Bring the tray to the table.
- Right away, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

143
00:09:46,750 --> 00:09:49,090
Right there. Have those been brushed and blocked?

144
00:09:49,290 --> 00:09:51,090
- Of course, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.
- Pack them in the hat boxes.

145
00:09:51,170 --> 00:09:52,420
Is that from Oberstdorf & Company?

146
00:09:52,510 --> 00:09:53,460
I believe so, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

147
00:09:53,550 --> 00:09:55,340
- Second trunk.
- Who has the tickets?
- I do, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

148
00:09:55,420 --> 00:09:56,360
Give them to me.

149
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:00,600
These are in order. Wait in the corner.

150
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:09,940
I'm not leaving.

151
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:11,190
I beg your pardon?

152
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:13,400
- I'm not leaving.
- Why not?

153
00:10:13,610 --> 00:10:15,530
- I'm frightened.
- Of what?

154
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:18,160
I fear this may be the last time we ever see each other.

155
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:19,620
Why on earth would that be the case?

156
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:22,030
Well, I can't put it into words, but I feel it.

157
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:23,490
For goodness sake, there's no reason...

158
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:24,830
for you to leave us if you'd...

159
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,460
Come with me.

160
00:10:26,580 --> 00:10:28,040
To fucking Lutz?

161
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:30,330
- Please.
- Give me your hand.

162
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:35,460
You've nothing to fear. You're always anxious before you travel.

163
00:10:35,550 --> 00:10:36,970
I admit, you appear to be suffering...

164
00:10:37,090 --> 00:10:38,470
a more acute attack on this occasion.

165
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:40,480
But, truly and honestly... Oh, dear God.

166
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:41,510
What have you done to your fingernails?

167
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:43,430
- I beg your pardon?
- This diabolical varnish.

168
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:45,520
- The color is completely wrong.
- Don't you like it?

169
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:48,520
It's not that I don't like it. I am physically repulsed.

170
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:51,060
Perhaps this will soothe you.

171
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:52,820
- What? Don't recite.

172
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:54,110
Just listen to the words.

173
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:55,320
- Hush.
- Please. Not now.

174
00:10:55,860 --> 00:10:59,410
"While questing once in noble wood of gray, medieval pine,

175
00:10:59,610 --> 00:11:03,780
"I came upon a tomb, rain-slick'd, rubbed-cool, ethereal,

176
00:11:03,990 --> 00:11:05,740
"its inscription long-vanished,

177
00:11:05,950 --> 00:11:08,370
"yet still within its melancholy fissures..."

178
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:11,000
MADAME D.: Will you light a candle for me, please?

179
00:11:12,330 --> 00:11:14,040
In the sacristy of Santa Maria?

180
00:11:14,170 --> 00:11:15,880
GUSTAVE H: I'll see to it myself immediately.

181
00:11:16,590 --> 00:11:20,010
Remember, I'm always with you.

182
00:11:20,970 --> 00:11:22,260
I love you.

183
00:11:22,430 --> 00:11:23,720
I love you.

184
00:11:24,010 --> 00:11:25,470
(SPEAKS GERMAN)

185
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:37,820
It's quite a thing winning the loyalty of a woman like that...

186
00:11:38,030 --> 00:11:40,030
for 19 consecutive seasons.

187
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:42,200
Um... Yes, sir.

188
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:44,160
She's very fond of me, you know.

189
00:11:44,330 --> 00:11:45,330
Yes, sir.

190
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:47,450
But I've never seen her like that before.

191
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:48,660
No, sir.

192
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:51,120
She was shaking like a shitting dog.

193
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:53,540
Truly.

194
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:56,130
Run to the cathedral of Santa Maria in Brucknerplatz.

195
00:11:56,340 --> 00:11:58,050
Buy one of the plain, half-length candles...

196
00:11:58,130 --> 00:11:59,590
and take back four Klubecks in change.

197
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:01,590
Light it in the sacristy, say a brief rosary...

198
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:03,930
then go to Mendi's and get me a <i>courtesan au chocolat.</i>

199
00:12:04,140 --> 00:12:06,600
If there's any money left, give it to the crippled shoe-shine boy.

200
00:12:08,470 --> 00:12:09,480
Right away, sir.

201
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:10,600
Hold it.

202
00:12:12,850 --> 00:12:14,650
Who are you?

203
00:12:14,770 --> 00:12:16,520
I'm Zero, sir. The new Lobby Boy.

204
00:12:16,730 --> 00:12:17,940
- Zero, you say?
- Yes, sir.

205
00:12:18,030 --> 00:12:19,400
I've never heard of you, never laid eyes on you.

206
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:20,490
Who hired you?

207
00:12:20,690 --> 00:12:21,820
Mr. Mosher, sir.

208
00:12:21,990 --> 00:12:23,110
Mr. Mosher!

209
00:12:24,410 --> 00:12:25,580
Yes, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave?

210
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:28,290
Am I to understand you've surreptitiously hired this young man...

211
00:12:28,370 --> 00:12:29,660
in the position of a Lobby Boy?

212
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:31,620
He's been engaged for a trial period,

213
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:33,460
pending your approval, of course.

214
00:12:33,540 --> 00:12:34,830
Uh...

215
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:37,340
Perhaps, yes. Thank you, Mr. Mosher.

216
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:39,000
You're most welcome, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

217
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:42,680
You're now going to be officially interviewed.

218
00:12:42,880 --> 00:12:45,050
Should I go and light the candle first, sir?

219
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:46,510
What? No.

220
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:51,310
Experience?

221
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:53,640
Hotel Kinski, Kitchen Boy, six months.

222
00:12:53,850 --> 00:12:56,020
Hotel Berlitz, Mop and Broom Boy, three months.

223
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:57,570
Before that I was a Skillet Scrubber...

224
00:12:57,690 --> 00:12:59,020
Experience, zero.

225
00:12:59,110 --> 00:13:00,110
Thank you again, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

226
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:01,190
Straighten that cap, Anatole.

227
00:13:01,280 --> 00:13:03,150
- The pleasure's mine, <i>Herr</i> Schneider.
- The strap's busted.

228
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:05,110
- These are not acceptable.
- I fully agree.

229
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,080
Education?

230
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:07,910
I studied reading and spelling.

231
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:10,370
I started my primary school. I almost...

232
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:11,580
Education, zero.

233
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:12,750
Now it's exploded.

234
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:15,000
Good morning, Cicero. Call the goddamn plumber!

235
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:16,210
This afternoon, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave?

236
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:17,500
Without fail, <i>Frau</i> Liebling.

237
00:13:17,590 --> 00:13:19,170
- What in hell is this?
- Not now.

238
00:13:19,340 --> 00:13:20,340
Family?

239
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:24,590
Zero.

240
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:27,000
Six, Igor.

241
00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:37,060
Why do you want to be a Lobby Boy?

242
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:43,530
Well, who wouldn't, at the Grand Budapest, sir?

243
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:45,740
It's an institution.

244
00:13:48,620 --> 00:13:50,200
Very good.

245
00:14:05,470 --> 00:14:06,510
A thousand Klubecks.

246
00:14:06,630 --> 00:14:07,680
My goodness.

247
00:14:08,970 --> 00:14:10,560
Were you ever a Lobby Boy, sir?

248
00:14:11,930 --> 00:14:12,930
What do you think?

249
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:15,060
Well, I suppose you'd have to start somewhere...

250
00:14:15,270 --> 00:14:16,940
- Go and light the goddamn candle.
- Yes, sir.

251
00:14:22,150 --> 00:14:24,150
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>And so, my life began.</i>

252
00:14:26,990 --> 00:14:28,280
<i>Junior Lobby Boy in-training,</i>

253
00:14:28,490 --> 00:14:29,910
<i>Grand Budapest Hotel,</i>

254
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:32,560
<i>under the strict command of</i> Monsieur <i>Gustave H.</i>

255
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,620
<i>I became his pupil, and he was to be my counselor and guardian.</i>

256
00:14:36,830 --> 00:14:38,540
GUSTAVE H: <i>What is a Lobby Boy?</i>

257
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:42,130
<i>A Lobby Boy's completely invisible, yet always in sight.</i>

258
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:44,380
<i>A Lobby Boy remembers what people hate.</i>

259
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:46,380
<i>A Lobby Boy anticipates the client's needs...</i>

260
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:48,680
<i>before the needs are needed.</i>

261
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:51,970
<i>A Lobby Boy is, above all, discreet to a fault.</i>

262
00:14:52,180 --> 00:14:54,260
Our guests know their deepest secrets,

263
00:14:54,350 --> 00:14:55,930
some of which are, frankly, rather unseemly,

264
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:57,850
will go with us to our graves.

265
00:14:58,060 --> 00:14:59,310
So keep your mouth shut, Zero.

266
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:00,220
Yes, sir.

267
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:04,780
That's all for now.

268
00:15:04,980 --> 00:15:07,000
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>I began to realize that many of the hotel's...</i>

269
00:15:07,010 --> 00:15:10,820
<i>most valued and distinguished guests came for him.</i>

270
00:15:11,030 --> 00:15:12,660
<i>It seemed to be an essential part of his duties...</i>

271
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:14,080
Ah! (LAUGHS)

272
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:16,410
<i>...but I believe it was also his pleasure.</i>

273
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:20,460
<i>The requirements were always the same.</i>

274
00:15:20,670 --> 00:15:23,130
<i>They had to be rich,</i>

275
00:15:23,340 --> 00:15:24,420
<i>old,</i>

276
00:15:24,630 --> 00:15:25,800
<i>insecure,</i>

277
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,050
<i>vain,</i>

278
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:28,510
<i>superficial,</i>

279
00:15:28,720 --> 00:15:29,800
<i>blonde,</i>

280
00:15:30,010 --> 00:15:31,010
<i>needy.</i>

281
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:34,220
Why blonde?

282
00:15:36,850 --> 00:15:38,140
Because they all were.

283
00:15:38,430 --> 00:15:39,730
<i>He was, by the way,</i>

284
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,520
<i>the most liberally perfumed man I had ever encountered.</i>

285
00:15:43,730 --> 00:15:46,570
<i>The scent announced his approach from a great distance</i>

286
00:15:48,070 --> 00:15:50,570
<i>and lingered for many minutes after he was gone.</i>

287
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:51,910
(MAN SNIFFING)

288
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:55,450
<i>I worked six days each week plus a half-day Sunday,</i>

289
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:57,910
<i>5:00 AM until just after midnight.</i>

290
00:15:59,080 --> 00:16:01,670
<i>Our meals were small but frequent, for stamina.</i>

291
00:16:01,870 --> 00:16:04,670
<i>Two breakfasts, two lunches and a late supper.</i>

292
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:09,090
Monsieur <i>Gustave also delivered a nightly sermon.</i>

293
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:12,380
Rudeness is merely the expression of fear.

294
00:16:12,590 --> 00:16:14,800
People fear they won't get what they want.

295
00:16:15,010 --> 00:16:18,520
The most dreadful and unattractive person only needs to be loved,

296
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:21,140
and they will open up like a flower.

297
00:16:21,350 --> 00:16:22,940
I am reminded of a verse,

298
00:16:23,150 --> 00:16:25,560
"The painter's brush touched the inchoate face..

299
00:16:25,770 --> 00:16:27,730
"by ends of nimble bristles...

300
00:16:27,940 --> 00:16:29,820
"and with their blush of first color,

301
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:31,820
"rendered her lifeless cheek living."

302
00:16:32,030 --> 00:16:35,070
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>His own dinner, he took alone in his room.</i>

303
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:44,460
<i>The identity of the owner of the hotel was unknown to all of us.</i>

304
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:48,460
<i>Each month, his emissary, known as Deputy Kovacs, arrived</i>

305
00:16:48,670 --> 00:16:50,510
<i>to review the books and convey messages...</i>

306
00:16:50,710 --> 00:16:53,220
<i>on behalf of the mysterious proprietor.</i>

307
00:16:53,430 --> 00:16:56,390
<i>On these occasions,</i> Monsieur <i>Gustave and our business manager,</i>

308
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:00,100
Herr <i>Becker, met with him in private consultation above Reception.</i>

309
00:17:00,180 --> 00:17:01,180
(INAUDIBLE)

310
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:15,360
<i>This was also when I met Agatha,</i>

311
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:35,550
<i>but we won't discuss that.</i>

312
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:27,480
- What do you want?
- Look.

313
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:33,730
GUSTAVE H: Dear God.

314
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:35,190
I'm terribly sorry, sir.

315
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:36,400
We must go to her.

316
00:18:36,490 --> 00:18:37,490
We must?

317
00:18:37,610 --> 00:18:39,420
<i>Tout de suite.</i> She needs me, and I need...

318
00:18:39,450 --> 00:18:41,160
you to help me with my bags and so on.

319
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:43,040
(WOMAN AND GUSTAVE H SPEAKING FRENCH)

320
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:44,830
- How fast can you pack?
- Five minutes.

321
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:47,000
Do it. And bring a bottle of the Pouilly-Jouvet '26...

322
00:18:47,080 --> 00:18:48,290
in an ice bucket with two glasses...

323
00:18:48,370 --> 00:18:51,340
so we don't have to drink the cat piss they serve in the dining car.

324
00:19:00,390 --> 00:19:02,510
I blame myself.

325
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:06,560
She tried to tell me she had a premonition. I didn't listen.

326
00:19:06,770 --> 00:19:08,350
All of Lutz will be dressed in black,

327
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:10,480
except her own ghastly, deceitful children

328
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:12,730
whom she loathed and couldn't bear to kiss hello.

329
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:15,230
They'll be dancing like gypsies.

330
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:20,320
There's really no point in doing anything in life,

331
00:19:20,410 --> 00:19:22,280
because it's all over in the blink of an eye...

332
00:19:22,410 --> 00:19:25,370
And, the next thing you know, rigor mortis sets in.

333
00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:28,410
Oh, how the good die young.

334
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:31,580
With any luck, she's left a few Klubecks for your old friend,

335
00:19:31,750 --> 00:19:33,340
but one never knows until the ink

336
00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:35,050
is dry on the death certificate.

337
00:19:36,550 --> 00:19:38,380
She was dynamite in the sack, by the way.

338
00:19:40,430 --> 00:19:42,760
She was 84, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

339
00:19:42,930 --> 00:19:44,430
I've had older.

340
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:46,220
When you're young, it's all fillet steak,

341
00:19:46,430 --> 00:19:49,430
but as the years go by, you have to move on to the cheaper cuts,

342
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,900
which is fine with me, because I like those.

343
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,360
More flavorful, or so they say.

344
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:56,070
(BRAKES SCREECHING)

345
00:19:57,730 --> 00:19:59,740
Why are we stopping at a barley field?

346
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:16,670
Well, hello there, chaps.

347
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:18,300
Documents, please.

348
00:20:18,510 --> 00:20:20,510
With pleasure.

349
00:20:24,590 --> 00:20:26,470
It's not a very flattering portrait, I'm afraid.

350
00:20:26,680 --> 00:20:28,310
I was once considered a great beauty.

351
00:20:29,640 --> 00:20:31,980
What does the "F" stand for? Fritz? Franz?

352
00:20:32,190 --> 00:20:33,440
Franz.

353
00:20:33,560 --> 00:20:34,770
I knew it!

354
00:20:36,150 --> 00:20:38,020
He's making a funny face.

355
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:41,820
That's a Migratory Visa with Stage Three Worker Status, Franz, darling.

356
00:20:41,990 --> 00:20:44,110
He's with me.

357
00:20:44,950 --> 00:20:45,990
Come outside, please.

358
00:20:47,490 --> 00:20:50,450
Now, wait a minute. Sit down, Zero. His papers are in order.

359
00:20:50,660 --> 00:20:52,120
I cross-referenced them myself with...

360
00:20:52,250 --> 00:20:53,670
the Bureau of Labor and Servitude.

361
00:20:53,870 --> 00:20:55,760
You can't arrest him simply because he's a bloody immigrant.

362
00:20:55,830 --> 00:20:57,200
He hasn't done anything wrong.

363
00:21:03,630 --> 00:21:05,550
Stop it, damn you!

364
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:07,850
Never mind, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave! Let them proceed!

365
00:21:08,050 --> 00:21:09,680
GUSTAVE H: Ow! That hurts!

366
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:12,430
(GROANING)

367
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:15,310
You filthy, goddamn, pock-marked, fascist assholes!

368
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:17,810
Take your hands off my Lobby Boy!

369
00:21:17,900 --> 00:21:19,940
(WHISTLE TOOTING)

370
00:21:20,030 --> 00:21:21,490
(DOOR OPENS)

371
00:21:29,700 --> 00:21:31,040
What's the problem?

372
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:32,290
This is outrageous.

373
00:21:32,370 --> 00:21:35,040
The young man works for me at the Grand Budapest Hotel in Nebelsbad.

374
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:38,380
<i>Monsieur</i> Gustave?

375
00:21:41,210 --> 00:21:43,220
My name is Henckels.

376
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:47,590
I'm the son of Dr. and Mrs. Wolfgang Henckels-Bergersdörfer.

377
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:49,550
Do you remember me?

378
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:54,230
I know exactly who you are. It's uncanny. You're little Albert.

379
00:21:54,390 --> 00:21:56,230
I'm terribly embarrassed. Release them.

380
00:21:57,100 --> 00:21:59,230
Release them.

381
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:05,240
Hmm.

382
00:22:06,030 --> 00:22:07,280
Your colleague is stateless.

383
00:22:07,530 --> 00:22:09,740
He'll need to apply for a revised Special Transit Permit,

384
00:22:09,950 --> 00:22:13,250
which at this point may be very difficult to acquire.

385
00:22:13,410 --> 00:22:14,410
Take this.

386
00:22:16,080 --> 00:22:17,460
It's temporary

387
00:22:17,540 --> 00:22:18,960
but it's the best I can offer, I'm afraid.

388
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:21,210
And how's your wonderful mother?

389
00:22:21,420 --> 00:22:23,340
- She's very well, thank you.
- I adore her.

390
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:25,720
- Send my love.
- I will.

391
00:22:26,630 --> 00:22:28,470
Your companion was very kind to me...

392
00:22:28,590 --> 00:22:30,470
when I was a lonely little boy.

393
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:33,970
My men and I apologize for disturbing you.

394
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:36,230
I beg your pardon, sir.

395
00:22:44,190 --> 00:22:45,610
(TRAIN DOOR CLOSES)

396
00:22:47,110 --> 00:22:48,610
(TRAIN CHUGGING)

397
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:55,790
You see? There are still faint glimmers of civilization...

398
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:57,790
left in this barbaric slaughterhouse...

399
00:22:57,910 --> 00:22:59,670
that was once known as humanity.

400
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:01,290
Indeed, that's what we provide...

401
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:04,170
in our own modest, humble, insignificant...

402
00:23:05,670 --> 00:23:07,670
Oh, fuck it.

403
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,790
Where is she, Clotilde? Take me to her.

404
00:24:17,370 --> 00:24:19,370
You're looking so well, darling. You really are.

405
00:24:19,580 --> 00:24:20,910
They've done a marvelous job.

406
00:24:21,160 --> 00:24:22,290
I don't know what sort of cream...

407
00:24:22,420 --> 00:24:23,540
they've put on you down at the morgue,

408
00:24:23,750 --> 00:24:25,040
but I want some.

409
00:24:25,250 --> 00:24:27,500
Honestly, you look better than you have in years.

410
00:24:27,710 --> 00:24:29,710
You look like you're alive.

411
00:24:35,390 --> 00:24:39,220
Oh, you changed it after all. It's perfect.

412
00:24:39,430 --> 00:24:41,230
- Clotilde?
- <i>Oui, Monsieur</i> Gustave?

413
00:24:41,390 --> 00:24:43,440
A glass of chilled water with no ice, please.

414
00:24:43,850 --> 00:24:44,940
Yes, M. Gustave--

415
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:48,230
and, also, M. Serge would like to speak with you privately in his office, please.

416
00:24:49,230 --> 00:24:50,320
Oh.

417
00:24:50,690 --> 00:24:52,400
All right, then.

418
00:24:52,610 --> 00:24:54,610
I shan't be long, darling.

419
00:24:55,870 --> 00:24:57,910
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>We were escorted through a green baize door,</i>

420
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:01,450
<i>down a narrow service corridor and into the butler's pantry.</i>

421
00:25:04,370 --> 00:25:06,460
<i>A moment later, the kitchen passage swung open</i>

422
00:25:06,580 --> 00:25:09,920
<i>and a small servant dressed in white jolted into the room.</i>

423
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:13,090
<i>I've never forgotten the look on that man's face.</i>

424
00:25:13,930 --> 00:25:15,660
What the devil is going on?

425
00:25:16,550 --> 00:25:18,350
<i>I, myself, had never set foot inside</i>

426
00:25:18,470 --> 00:25:20,270
<i>a house of this kind in my life.</i>

427
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:24,230
<i>I understood very little about the events that were to follow.</i>

428
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:27,400
<i>But, eventually, I came to recognize,</i>

429
00:25:27,610 --> 00:25:31,110
<i>when the destiny of a great fortune is at stake,</i>

430
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:35,070
<i>men's greed spreads like a poison in the bloodstream.</i>

431
00:25:38,410 --> 00:25:40,580
<i>Uncles, nephews, cousins,</i>

432
00:25:40,790 --> 00:25:43,410
<i>in-laws of increasingly tenuous connection.</i>

433
00:25:43,620 --> 00:25:45,120
<i>The old woman's most distant relations...</i>

434
00:25:45,330 --> 00:25:47,460
<i>had come foraging out of the woodwork.</i>

435
00:25:49,090 --> 00:25:51,090
<i>At the head of this congregation,</i>

436
00:25:51,210 --> 00:25:53,260
<i>it was a disorienting coincidence,</i>

437
00:25:53,470 --> 00:25:56,180
<i>we discovered our own Deputy Kovacs,</i>

438
00:25:56,430 --> 00:25:59,140
<i>himself an important attorney, of course.</i>

439
00:25:59,350 --> 00:26:02,680
<i>He was the executor of the dead widow's estate.</i>

440
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:12,530
This is Madame D's last will and testament.

441
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:15,820
It consists of a general tontine

442
00:26:15,990 --> 00:26:19,870
drawn up before the event of her husband's death 46 years ago,

443
00:26:20,120 --> 00:26:23,290
in combination with 635 amendments,

444
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:26,160
notations, corrections, and letters of wishes

445
00:26:26,370 --> 00:26:29,460
executed during the subsequent decades.

446
00:26:31,710 --> 00:26:33,460
The ultimate legality of this accumulation...

447
00:26:33,670 --> 00:26:34,840
requires further analysis,

448
00:26:35,010 --> 00:26:38,050
but in the opinion of this office, it was Madame D's intention...

449
00:26:38,300 --> 00:26:40,180
that control of the vast bulk of her estate...

450
00:26:40,350 --> 00:26:43,010
should be transferred, forthwith, to her son, Dmitri,

451
00:26:43,220 --> 00:26:45,850
with special allowances for his sisters,

452
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:48,480
Marguerite, Laetizia, and Carolina,

453
00:26:48,690 --> 00:26:51,690
and minor gifts for various members of the extended family

454
00:26:51,900 --> 00:26:53,610
as shown in the List of Recipients,

455
00:26:53,730 --> 00:26:55,400
which I will elucidate in due course.

456
00:26:55,990 --> 00:26:57,650
(ALL MURMURING)

457
00:26:57,740 --> 00:26:58,860
However.

458
00:27:02,410 --> 00:27:03,700
An additional codicil,

459
00:27:03,870 --> 00:27:07,210
delivered into my possession by post only this morning,

460
00:27:07,370 --> 00:27:09,750
and, by all indications, sent by Madame D

461
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:11,250
during the last hours of her life,

462
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:14,000
contains an amendment to the original certificate,

463
00:27:14,210 --> 00:27:18,220
which, as prescribed by law, I will read to you now.

464
00:27:18,260 --> 00:27:19,650
The authenticity of this document...

465
00:27:19,660 --> 00:27:21,680
has not yet been confirmed by the presiding magistrate,

466
00:27:21,890 --> 00:27:26,060
so I ask that all parties be patient and refrain from comment...

467
00:27:26,270 --> 00:27:29,560
until such time as our investigations can be completed.

468
00:27:38,300 --> 00:27:41,740
"To my esteemed friend who comforted me in my later years...

469
00:27:41,910 --> 00:27:44,240
"and brought sunshine into the life of an old woman...

470
00:27:44,450 --> 00:27:47,250
"who thought that she would never be happy again.

471
00:27:47,410 --> 00:27:49,290
<i>"Monsieur</i> Gustave H,

472
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:53,420
"I bequeath, bestow and devise, free of all taxation...

473
00:27:53,590 --> 00:27:56,420
"and with full and absolute fiduciary entitlement,

474
00:27:57,420 --> 00:27:59,090
"the painting known as <i>'Boy with Apple..."</i>

475
00:27:59,260 --> 00:28:00,970
- Wow!
- "...by Johannes van Hoytl..."

476
00:28:01,220 --> 00:28:02,590
- I can't believe it.
- "...the younger..."

477
00:28:02,760 --> 00:28:03,890
What?

478
00:28:04,010 --> 00:28:05,140
"...which gave us both so much pleasure."

479
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:06,260
The van Hoytl?

480
00:28:06,350 --> 00:28:07,470
- Tax-free?
- Can she do that?

481
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:09,730
Who's Gustave H?

482
00:28:10,940 --> 00:28:12,600
I'm afraid that's me, darling.

483
00:28:14,940 --> 00:28:17,280
That fucking faggot!

484
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:19,940
He's a concierge. What are you doing here?

485
00:28:20,110 --> 00:28:23,110
I've come to pay my respects to a great woman whom I loved.

486
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:24,910
This man is an intruder in my home!

487
00:28:25,030 --> 00:28:26,580
It's not yours yet, Dmitri.

488
00:28:26,660 --> 00:28:29,110
Only when probate is granted, and the Deed of Entitlement...

489
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:31,460
You're not getting <i>Boy with Apple,</i> you goddamn little fruit!

490
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:35,290
How's that supposed to make me feel?

491
00:28:35,460 --> 00:28:37,500
Call the police. We're pressing charges.

492
00:28:37,750 --> 00:28:40,760
This criminal has plagued my family for nearly 20 years.

493
00:28:40,970 --> 00:28:42,930
He's a ruthless adventurer and a con-artist...

494
00:28:43,010 --> 00:28:45,140
who preys on mentally feeble, sick old ladies,

495
00:28:45,350 --> 00:28:47,430
and he probably fucks them, too!

496
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:49,770
I go to bed with all my friends.

497
00:28:50,140 --> 00:28:51,230
(CROWD EXCLAIMS)

498
00:28:52,060 --> 00:28:53,230
(CROWD EXCLAIMS LOUDER)

499
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:55,400
(CROWD SHRIEKS)

500
00:28:59,150 --> 00:29:01,320
Where's Céline?

501
00:29:01,490 --> 00:29:02,300
- (SPITS DRINK)
- What?

502
00:29:02,990 --> 00:29:05,030
She's dead. We're reading her will.

503
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:07,490
Oh, yes, yes, of course.

504
00:29:07,700 --> 00:29:10,540
If I learn you ever once laid a finger on my mother's body,

505
00:29:10,790 --> 00:29:12,370
living or dead, I swear to God,

506
00:29:12,500 --> 00:29:14,120
I'll cut your throat! You hear me?

507
00:29:14,330 --> 00:29:16,460
I thought I was supposed to be a fucking faggot.

508
00:29:17,540 --> 00:29:18,750
You are, but you're bisexual.

509
00:29:18,880 --> 00:29:20,130
Let's change the subject.

510
00:29:20,340 --> 00:29:21,380
I'm leaving.

511
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:30,260
Wait here quietly, please.

512
00:29:30,470 --> 00:29:33,690
That picture, <i>Boy with Apple,</i> is priceless. Understand?

513
00:29:33,890 --> 00:29:35,640
Congratulations, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave!

514
00:29:35,650 --> 00:29:37,360
They're going to fight me for the son of a bitch.

515
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:38,650
Is it very beautiful?

516
00:29:38,770 --> 00:29:39,860
Beyond description.

517
00:29:40,070 --> 00:29:42,530
"E'en the most gifted bard's rhyme can only sing...

518
00:29:42,690 --> 00:29:44,740
"but to the lack of her and all she isn't!

519
00:29:44,990 --> 00:29:46,360
- "His tongue doth..."
- Can I see it?

520
00:29:47,570 --> 00:29:48,680
I don't see why not.

521
00:30:21,190 --> 00:30:23,610
This is van Hoytl's exquisite portrayal...

522
00:30:23,860 --> 00:30:26,070
of a beautiful boy on the cusp of manhood.

523
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:28,910
Blond, smooth. Skin as white as that milk.

524
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:30,450
Of impeccable provenance.

525
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:34,200
One of the last in private hands, and unquestionably, the best.

526
00:30:34,410 --> 00:30:35,710
It's a masterpiece.

527
00:30:35,910 --> 00:30:37,960
The rest of this shit is worthless junk.

528
00:31:25,050 --> 00:31:26,160
<i>M. Gustave?</i>

529
00:31:29,300 --> 00:31:30,560
Can I help you?

530
00:31:32,140 --> 00:31:33,010
Yes, Serge.

531
00:31:33,010 --> 00:31:34,930
You can wrap this up, please.

532
00:31:35,180 --> 00:31:36,310
Wrap up--

533
00:31:36,310 --> 00:31:37,980
Wrap up-- "Boy With Apple"?

534
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:59,500
What did you want to tell me, before?

535
00:32:00,830 --> 00:32:02,830
I think I cannot say right now.

536
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:05,340
Write me tomorrow. Lutzbahn Station!

537
00:32:17,310 --> 00:32:19,310
I'll never part with it.

538
00:32:19,520 --> 00:32:23,900
It reminded her of me. It will remind me of her. Always.

539
00:32:24,150 --> 00:32:26,820
I'll die with this picture above my bed.

540
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:29,030
See the resemblance?

541
00:32:30,030 --> 00:32:32,030
Oh, yes.

542
00:32:37,370 --> 00:32:39,080
Actually, we should sell it.

543
00:32:39,330 --> 00:32:42,330
Sooner rather than later, in case they try to steal it back.

544
00:32:42,540 --> 00:32:43,830
Plus, something about those lunatic...

545
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:45,250
foot-soldiers on the express...

546
00:32:45,500 --> 00:32:48,880
This could be a tricky war and a long dry spell in the hotel trade.

547
00:32:49,050 --> 00:32:51,590
For all we know, they could board us up tomorrow.

548
00:32:57,930 --> 00:32:59,890
Let's make a solemn blood-pact.

549
00:33:00,100 --> 00:33:02,390
We'll contact the black market and liquidate <i>Boy with Apple</i>...

550
00:33:02,600 --> 00:33:03,500
by the end of the week,

551
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:05,060
then leave the country and lay low...

552
00:33:05,230 --> 00:33:06,610
somewhere along the Maltese Riviera...

553
00:33:06,860 --> 00:33:09,360
until the troubles blow over and we resume our posts.

554
00:33:09,570 --> 00:33:11,280
In exchange for your help, your loyalty...

555
00:33:11,530 --> 00:33:13,280
and your services as my personal valet,

556
00:33:13,530 --> 00:33:18,030
I pledge to you 1.5% of the net sale price.

557
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:20,240
- 1.5?
- Plus room and board.

558
00:33:21,680 --> 00:33:24,120
- Could we make it 10?
- 10? Are you joking?

559
00:33:24,370 --> 00:33:26,180
That's more than I'd pay an actual dealer,

560
00:33:26,250 --> 00:33:28,590
and you wouldn't know chiaroscuro from chicken giblets.

561
00:33:28,790 --> 00:33:30,660
No, 1.5 is correct. But I'll tell you what,

562
00:33:30,710 --> 00:33:33,980
if I die first, and I most certainly will, you will be my sole heir.

563
00:33:34,090 --> 00:33:35,430
There's not much in the kitty except...

564
00:33:35,550 --> 00:33:36,990
a set of ivory-backed hairbrushes...

565
00:33:37,090 --> 00:33:38,560
and my library of romantic poetry,

566
00:33:38,640 --> 00:33:41,270
but when the time comes, these will be yours,

567
00:33:41,470 --> 00:33:44,770
along with whatever we haven't already spent on whores and whiskey.

568
00:33:44,940 --> 00:33:46,260
This is our sacred bond.

569
00:33:47,770 --> 00:33:49,080
I'll draw it up right now.

570
00:33:54,780 --> 00:33:58,820
I, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave H, being of relatively sound mind and body,

571
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:02,910
on this day, the 19th of October, in the year of our Lord 1932...

572
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:05,160
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>He never told me where he came from.</i>

573
00:34:09,420 --> 00:34:11,670
<i>I never asked who his family had been.</i>

574
00:34:32,610 --> 00:34:33,510
Excuse me.

575
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:36,280
Uh-huh?

576
00:34:37,450 --> 00:34:40,560
The police are here. They asked for you.

577
00:34:44,700 --> 00:34:46,210
Tell them I'll be right down.

578
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:48,500
Okay.

579
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:56,880
Have you ever been questioned by the authorities?

580
00:34:56,970 --> 00:34:58,220
Yes, on one occasion I was arrested

581
00:34:58,340 --> 00:34:59,640
and tortured by the rebel militia...

582
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:00,890
after the Desert Uprising.

583
00:35:01,140 --> 00:35:03,280
- You know the drill, then. Zip it.
- Of course.

584
00:35:03,310 --> 00:35:05,160
- You've never heard the word "van Hoytl" in your life.
- Got it.

585
00:35:05,180 --> 00:35:06,080
Okay, let's go.

586
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:23,700
How may we serve you, gentlemen?

587
00:35:25,040 --> 00:35:26,360
Ah, Inspector Henckels.

588
00:35:26,370 --> 00:35:28,910
"By order of the Commissioner of Police, Zubrowka Province,

589
00:35:29,170 --> 00:35:30,880
"I hereby place you under arrest for the murder...

590
00:35:31,080 --> 00:35:33,540
"of Madame Céline Villeneuve Desgoffe und Taxis."

591
00:35:33,710 --> 00:35:35,210
I knew there was something fishy.

592
00:35:35,340 --> 00:35:36,840
We never got the cause of death.

593
00:35:37,050 --> 00:35:40,220
She's been murdered and you think I did it.

594
00:35:41,890 --> 00:35:42,890
HENCKELS: Hey!

595
00:35:45,720 --> 00:35:46,730
Stop!

596
00:36:11,250 --> 00:36:12,750
(DOOR BANGING)

597
00:36:12,830 --> 00:36:14,250
(MAN WHISTLES)

598
00:36:36,900 --> 00:36:38,260
What happened?

599
00:36:39,150 --> 00:36:41,760
What happened, my dear Zero, is I beat the living shit...

600
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:44,650
out of a sniveling little runt called Pinky Bandinski...

601
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:46,580
who had the gall to question my virility,

602
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:48,910
because if there's one thing we've learned from penny dreadfuls,

603
00:36:49,120 --> 00:36:51,120
it's that, when you find yourself in a place like this,

604
00:36:51,330 --> 00:36:52,980
you must never be a candy-ass.

605
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:54,960
You've got to prove yourself from Day One.

606
00:36:55,130 --> 00:36:56,760
You've got to win their respect.

607
00:36:56,960 --> 00:37:00,090
You should take a long look at his ugly mug this morning.

608
00:37:03,430 --> 00:37:05,260
He's, actually, become a dear friend.

609
00:37:05,470 --> 00:37:06,970
You'll meet him, I hope.

610
00:37:07,970 --> 00:37:10,020
So. You've talked to Kovacs?

611
00:37:10,270 --> 00:37:11,640
I saw him last night in secret.

612
00:37:11,850 --> 00:37:14,020
He made me take an oath on a Bible I wouldn't tell a soul.

613
00:37:14,270 --> 00:37:15,350
You're supposed to also.

614
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:16,650
I'll do that later.

615
00:37:16,810 --> 00:37:17,980
He suspects you're innocent.

616
00:37:18,150 --> 00:37:19,210
Of course he does.

617
00:37:19,480 --> 00:37:21,280
What's the charge?

618
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:25,660
In the small hours of the evening of 19 October,

619
00:37:25,860 --> 00:37:28,910
an individual well-known to the house and staff, a <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave H,

620
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:32,250
did arrive at the Desgoffe und Taxis residence in Lutz...

621
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:34,960
and entered by the rear service alley,

622
00:37:35,170 --> 00:37:36,500
alerting no one to his presence,

623
00:37:36,710 --> 00:37:40,460
and did then proceed by way of back stairs and servants' passage,

624
00:37:40,670 --> 00:37:43,010
to deliver himself into the private chambers of Madame D.

625
00:37:43,220 --> 00:37:45,760
There is no evidence to indicate whether this visit...

626
00:37:45,970 --> 00:37:48,430
had been pre-arranged with her or not.

627
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:53,520
The next morning Madame D was found dead by strychnine poisoning.

628
00:37:53,730 --> 00:37:55,850
<i>Monsieur</i> Gustave was not observed on the premises again...

629
00:37:56,060 --> 00:37:59,230
until, of course, 24 hours later.

630
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:01,690
The identity of his accusers is made...

631
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:04,110
clear in this notarized deposition.

632
00:38:04,320 --> 00:38:07,740
They include, essentially, all members of the extended family,

633
00:38:07,950 --> 00:38:12,660
but the key witness who actually ostensibly saw the alleged events...

634
00:38:12,870 --> 00:38:15,500
appears to have fled the jurisdiction.

635
00:38:15,710 --> 00:38:17,370
His whereabouts are currently unknown,

636
00:38:17,580 --> 00:38:20,210
but he's being sought and pursued by the relevant authorities.

637
00:38:20,960 --> 00:38:22,000
Who is he?

638
00:38:26,630 --> 00:38:27,640
Serge?

639
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:28,760
I'm afraid so.

640
00:38:28,970 --> 00:38:30,480
That little prick.

641
00:38:31,430 --> 00:38:33,560
No, I don't believe it. They put him up to it.

642
00:38:33,770 --> 00:38:35,890
I've been dropped into a nest of vipers.

643
00:38:36,100 --> 00:38:37,100
You have an alibi?

644
00:38:37,310 --> 00:38:39,480
Of course, but she's married to the Duke of Westphalia.

645
00:38:39,690 --> 00:38:42,320
I can't allow her name to get mixed-up in all this monkey business.

646
00:38:42,520 --> 00:38:43,860
Your life may be at stake.

647
00:38:43,900 --> 00:38:46,320
I know, but the bitch legged it.

648
00:38:46,530 --> 00:38:50,280
She's already onboard the <i>Queen Nasstasja</i> halfway to Dutch Tanganyika.

649
00:38:52,790 --> 00:38:54,040
Don't give up.

650
00:38:59,880 --> 00:39:01,420
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>The details of the conspiracy,</i>

651
00:39:02,750 --> 00:39:04,880
<i>now a matter of public record,</i>

652
00:39:05,090 --> 00:39:08,220
<i>were, at that time, impossible for us to apprehend.</i>

653
00:39:18,770 --> 00:39:20,060
(UNLOCKING)

654
00:39:25,570 --> 00:39:27,110
I'm looking for Serge X,

655
00:39:27,480 --> 00:39:30,260
a young man in the service of my employer,

656
00:39:30,280 --> 00:39:33,870
the family Desgoffe und Taxis of Schloss Lutz.

657
00:39:35,490 --> 00:39:37,080
- Yes, sir?
- You're his sister?

658
00:39:37,870 --> 00:39:39,670
- Yes, sir.
- Seen him lately?

659
00:39:40,830 --> 00:39:42,960
- No, sir.
- No, sir?

660
00:39:44,040 --> 00:39:45,050
No, sir.

661
00:39:48,470 --> 00:39:51,260
I need to find him right away, for his own safety...

662
00:39:51,470 --> 00:39:53,010
and everybody else's.

663
00:39:53,220 --> 00:39:54,360
If he shows up...

664
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:57,980
- Yes, sir?
- Tell him Jopling says,

665
00:39:58,900 --> 00:39:59,900
"Come home."

666
00:40:03,480 --> 00:40:06,070
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>But one thing was certain,</i>

667
00:40:08,110 --> 00:40:11,700
<i>the Desgoffe und Taxis were a very powerful family,</i>

668
00:40:15,830 --> 00:40:18,040
<i>and time was not on our side.</i>

669
00:40:19,000 --> 00:40:20,290
(OVERLAPPING CHATTER)

670
00:40:22,210 --> 00:40:23,710
(KNOCKING)

671
00:40:26,040 --> 00:40:28,840
A letter from <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave. Zero.

672
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:31,660
- You want me to?
- Read it.

673
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:35,640
(CLEARS THROAT)

674
00:40:37,600 --> 00:40:39,560
"My dear and trusted colleagues..."

675
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:41,810
I miss you deeply as I write from the confines...

676
00:40:42,020 --> 00:40:45,020
of my regrettable and preposterous incarceration.

677
00:40:45,230 --> 00:40:47,320
Until I walk amongst you again as a free man,

678
00:40:47,520 --> 00:40:49,900
the Grand Budapest remains in your hands,

679
00:40:50,110 --> 00:40:52,280
as does its impeccable reputation.

680
00:40:52,490 --> 00:40:54,490
Keep it spotless and glorify it.

681
00:40:54,700 --> 00:40:57,030
Take extra special care of every little-bitty bit of it...

682
00:40:57,240 --> 00:40:59,330
as if I were watching over you like a hawk...

683
00:40:59,540 --> 00:41:03,000
with a horse whip in its talons, because I am.

684
00:41:03,210 --> 00:41:06,250
Should I discover a lapse of any variety during my absence,

685
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:10,090
I promise, swift and merciless justice will descend upon you.

686
00:41:10,300 --> 00:41:14,260
A great and noble house has been placed under your protection.

687
00:41:14,470 --> 00:41:17,100
Tell Zero if you see any funny business.

688
00:41:17,300 --> 00:41:19,430
"Your devoted <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave."

689
00:41:19,640 --> 00:41:21,890
Then there's a poem, but we might want to start on the soup

690
00:41:22,100 --> 00:41:23,480
since it's 46 stanzas.

691
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:24,600
(CUTLERY CLATTERS)

692
00:41:24,690 --> 00:41:28,440
"A moist, black ash dampens the filth of a dung-dark rat's nest

693
00:41:28,650 --> 00:41:30,730
"and mingles with the thick scent of wood rot

694
00:41:30,940 --> 00:41:32,740
"while the lark song of a guttersnipe..."

695
00:41:32,950 --> 00:41:35,200
JOPLING: I never trusted that butler.

696
00:41:35,410 --> 00:41:37,200
He's too honest.

697
00:41:37,320 --> 00:41:39,080
- DMITRI ON PHONE: <i>Too honest, you say?</i>
- JOPLING: Mmm-hmm.

698
00:41:39,280 --> 00:41:41,410
<i>Right, well, be that as it may,</i>

699
00:41:41,580 --> 00:41:43,580
<i>find him quick and make it snappy.</i>

700
00:41:59,890 --> 00:42:02,470
May I offer any of you inmates a plate of mush?

701
00:42:04,980 --> 00:42:06,400
No? Anyone?

702
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:08,860
You with the very large scar on your face?

703
00:42:17,880 --> 00:42:21,120
Try it. It's actually quite warm and nourishing this morning.

704
00:42:21,330 --> 00:42:22,370
It needs a dash of salt.

705
00:42:31,250 --> 00:42:32,200
Good day.

706
00:42:37,630 --> 00:42:39,640
Mush, gents? Any takers?

707
00:42:43,770 --> 00:42:45,310
Suit yourselves.

708
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:52,520
Rise and shine. Chop, chop.

709
00:42:54,110 --> 00:42:55,320
Good morning, Pinky.

710
00:43:01,120 --> 00:43:02,410
Mendl's again?

711
00:43:02,490 --> 00:43:03,700
Precisely.

712
00:43:03,910 --> 00:43:05,010
Who's got the throat slitter?

713
00:43:11,500 --> 00:43:12,920
Out of this world.

714
00:43:13,050 --> 00:43:14,420
GUSTAVE H: Mendl's is the best.

715
00:43:14,630 --> 00:43:16,760
- Well, back to work.
- <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave?

716
00:43:20,340 --> 00:43:21,260
Yes?

717
00:43:21,850 --> 00:43:23,010
(DOOR CLOSES)

718
00:43:25,560 --> 00:43:27,850
PINKY: Me and the boys talked it over.

719
00:43:28,060 --> 00:43:30,520
We think you're a really straight fellow.

720
00:43:30,730 --> 00:43:32,900
Well, I've never been accused of that before,

721
00:43:33,110 --> 00:43:35,070
but I appreciate the sentiment.

722
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:36,650
You're one of us now.

723
00:43:37,950 --> 00:43:39,910
What a lovely thing to say.

724
00:43:40,110 --> 00:43:41,990
Thank you, dear Pinky. Thank you, Günther.

725
00:43:42,200 --> 00:43:43,620
Thank you, Wolf.

726
00:43:43,830 --> 00:43:45,380
Anything else?

727
00:43:47,660 --> 00:43:48,710
Tell him, Ludwig.

728
00:43:50,670 --> 00:43:53,960
Checkpoint 19 ain't no two-bit hoosegow.

729
00:43:54,170 --> 00:43:57,420
You got broad-gauge iron bars on every door, vent and window.

730
00:43:57,630 --> 00:44:01,220
You got 72 guards on the floor and 16 more in the towers.

731
00:44:01,430 --> 00:44:05,720
You got a 325-foot drop into a moat full of crocodiles.

732
00:44:05,930 --> 00:44:07,890
But, like the best of them, it's got a soft spot,

733
00:44:08,100 --> 00:44:09,850
which in this case happens to take the form...

734
00:44:10,060 --> 00:44:12,190
of a storm-drain sewer system...

735
00:44:12,400 --> 00:44:14,690
dating from the time of the original rock fortification...

736
00:44:14,900 --> 00:44:16,940
way back in the Middle Ages.

737
00:44:17,150 --> 00:44:20,240
Now, nobody's saying it's a stroll down a tree-lined promenade...

738
00:44:20,450 --> 00:44:22,200
with a fine lady and a white poodle,

739
00:44:22,410 --> 00:44:26,030
but it's got what you'd call "vulnerability,"

740
00:44:26,240 --> 00:44:28,910
and that's our bread and butter.

741
00:44:29,750 --> 00:44:30,880
Take a look.

742
00:44:33,130 --> 00:44:34,060
Who drew this?

743
00:44:34,670 --> 00:44:36,750
What do you mean, "Who drew this?" I did.

744
00:44:36,960 --> 00:44:39,050
Very good. You've got a wonderful line, Ludwig.

745
00:44:39,260 --> 00:44:40,920
This shows great artistic promise.

746
00:44:41,130 --> 00:44:44,510
Question, how do you intend to penetrate this lowest rudiment?

747
00:44:44,720 --> 00:44:47,260
It's 25 inches of reinforced granite masonry, is it not?

748
00:44:47,470 --> 00:44:49,180
Digging with the throat-slitter,

749
00:44:49,390 --> 00:44:52,020
I expect this would take three to six months of continual effort,

750
00:44:52,230 --> 00:44:53,730
during which time several of our members

751
00:44:53,940 --> 00:44:55,360
will have been violently executed.

752
00:44:55,560 --> 00:44:57,900
You hit the nail on the head, Mr. Gustave.

753
00:44:58,110 --> 00:45:00,690
We got fake documents, second-hand street clothes,

754
00:45:00,900 --> 00:45:03,280
a rope ladder made of sticks and bunk-linens,

755
00:45:03,490 --> 00:45:05,370
but we need digging tools,

756
00:45:05,570 --> 00:45:08,540
which are proving hard to come by in this flop-house.

757
00:45:22,920 --> 00:45:24,470
YOUNG WRITER: <i>At this point in the story,</i>

758
00:45:24,680 --> 00:45:28,260
<i>the old man fell silent and pushed away his saddle of lamb.</i>

759
00:45:28,470 --> 00:45:31,310
<i>His eyes went blank as two stones.</i>

760
00:45:31,520 --> 00:45:33,520
<i>I could see he was in distress.</i>

761
00:45:33,730 --> 00:45:34,940
Are you ill, Mr. Moustafa?

762
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:36,270
<i>I finally asked.</i>

763
00:45:36,480 --> 00:45:37,560
Oh, dear me, no.

764
00:45:37,880 --> 00:45:38,770
<i>He said.</i>

765
00:45:38,980 --> 00:45:41,400
It's only that I don't know how to proceed.

766
00:45:42,610 --> 00:45:43,780
<i>He was crying.</i>

767
00:45:44,990 --> 00:45:46,490
You see, I never speak of Agatha,

768
00:45:46,700 --> 00:45:50,290
because even at the thought of her name...

769
00:45:50,490 --> 00:45:52,660
I'm unable to control my emotions.

770
00:45:56,330 --> 00:45:58,590
Well, I suppose there's no way around it.

771
00:45:59,880 --> 00:46:01,660
You see, she saved us.

772
00:46:02,510 --> 00:46:04,380
<i>On our third formal rendezvous,</i>

773
00:46:04,550 --> 00:46:07,470
<i>I had asked for her hand in marriage and she had agreed.</i>

774
00:46:07,550 --> 00:46:09,550
- Will you marry me?
- Yes.

775
00:46:11,350 --> 00:46:14,230
<i>We did not have 50 Klubecks between the two of us.</i>

776
00:46:14,430 --> 00:46:17,690
<i>No one knew, of course, but, then, who would have cared?</i>

777
00:46:17,900 --> 00:46:20,650
<i>We were each completely on our own in the world,</i>

778
00:46:21,780 --> 00:46:23,780
<i>and we were deeply in love.</i>

779
00:46:32,580 --> 00:46:34,410
- Here.
- Thank you.

780
00:46:34,620 --> 00:46:36,460
- It's a book.
- I see.

781
00:46:36,670 --> 00:46:38,170
<i>Romantic Poetry, Volume One.</i>

782
00:46:38,380 --> 00:46:39,500
<i>Monsieur</i> Gustave recommended it.

783
00:46:39,630 --> 00:46:40,710
I have a copy of my own as well.

784
00:46:40,920 --> 00:46:42,880
I ruined the surprise, I suppose.

785
00:46:43,000 --> 00:46:44,970
- I'll go ahead and open it, anyway.
- Okay.

786
00:46:48,430 --> 00:46:49,560
Read the inscription.

787
00:46:51,510 --> 00:46:53,720
(READING)

788
00:47:08,950 --> 00:47:10,030
MR. MOUSTAFA: Monsieur <i>Gustave insisted...</i>

789
00:47:10,240 --> 00:47:12,490
<i>on an immediate and thorough cross-examination...</i>

790
00:47:12,700 --> 00:47:14,370
She's charming. She's so charming.

791
00:47:14,540 --> 00:47:15,500
(CHUCKLES)

792
00:47:15,580 --> 00:47:18,000
<i>...during which he presented Agatha with a porcelain pendant...</i>

793
00:47:18,210 --> 00:47:21,130
<i>and five dozen individually tissue-wrapped white tulips...</i>

794
00:47:21,330 --> 00:47:23,460
<i>in a box the size of a child's coffin.</i>

795
00:47:23,670 --> 00:47:24,960
It's not right.

796
00:47:25,090 --> 00:47:26,380
I beg your pardon?

797
00:47:26,590 --> 00:47:27,760
Why is he sulking?

798
00:47:27,860 --> 00:47:29,960
She's my girlfriend. You can't just buy her things.

799
00:47:30,050 --> 00:47:33,390
I'm only interviewing this vision of loveliness on your behalf.

800
00:47:33,600 --> 00:47:36,180
Never be jealous in this life, Zero, not even for an instant.

801
00:47:36,390 --> 00:47:37,930
Is he flirting with you?

802
00:47:39,230 --> 00:47:40,230
Yes.

803
00:47:40,850 --> 00:47:42,360
I approve of this union.

804
00:47:42,560 --> 00:47:45,070
Agatha, my beauty, return to your beloved.

805
00:47:45,280 --> 00:47:46,570
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>Soon we learned...</i>

806
00:47:46,780 --> 00:47:48,490
GUSTAVE H: Blessings upon you both.

807
00:47:48,700 --> 00:47:50,410
<i>...not only was Agatha immensely skilled...</i>

808
00:47:50,610 --> 00:47:53,120
<i>with a palette knife and a butter-cream flourish...</i>

809
00:47:53,330 --> 00:47:55,330
- Mendl.
- Go.

810
00:47:56,490 --> 00:47:58,500
<i>...she was also very brave.</i>

811
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:05,050
<i>I believe she was born that way.</i>

812
00:48:24,440 --> 00:48:25,660
Something's missing.

813
00:48:26,360 --> 00:48:31,150
A crucial document, either misplaced or, conceivably, destroyed.

814
00:48:31,360 --> 00:48:33,870
I don't know what it contains, I don't know what it represents,

815
00:48:34,070 --> 00:48:35,450
I don't know what it is,

816
00:48:35,660 --> 00:48:38,750
but there are traces and shadows of it everywhere.

817
00:48:38,950 --> 00:48:40,660
Now, I don't want to alarm you,

818
00:48:40,680 --> 00:48:42,580
and I don't expect to see any significant change...

819
00:48:42,790 --> 00:48:44,210
in the magistrate's ultimate decision...

820
00:48:44,330 --> 00:48:45,790
vis-à-vis your own inheritance,

821
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:49,800
but, especially given the circumstances of the death,

822
00:48:50,010 --> 00:48:52,180
as well as the disappearance of the key witness...

823
00:48:52,340 --> 00:48:54,470
in the murder case, Serge X,

824
00:48:54,480 --> 00:48:56,440
I suggest that we immediately bring this matter...

825
00:48:56,450 --> 00:48:58,770
to the attention of the municipal inspector...

826
00:48:58,970 --> 00:49:00,730
so that there can be absolutely no question...

827
00:49:00,930 --> 00:49:03,310
of impropriety at any future date.

828
00:49:03,520 --> 00:49:05,270
- Agreed?
- Not agreed.

829
00:49:07,110 --> 00:49:09,190
- Not agreed?
- Not agreed.

830
00:49:11,900 --> 00:49:13,490
Can I ask you a question, Vilmos?

831
00:49:14,110 --> 00:49:15,160
Yes, Dmitri?

832
00:49:15,370 --> 00:49:16,660
Who are you working for?

833
00:49:17,080 --> 00:49:18,230
I beg your pardon?

834
00:49:18,240 --> 00:49:20,040
Who are you working for?

835
00:49:20,200 --> 00:49:22,000
I thought you're supposed to be our lawyer.

836
00:49:22,680 --> 00:49:23,740
Well, in point of fact,

837
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:25,580
I'm the executor of the estate.

838
00:49:25,790 --> 00:49:29,920
In this particular situation, I represent the deceased.

839
00:49:30,130 --> 00:49:31,130
Oh, yeah?

840
00:49:31,260 --> 00:49:34,010
Yeah. A provision for my fees was included in the...

841
00:49:34,220 --> 00:49:39,010
Just wrap it up and don't make waves. Agreed?

842
00:49:41,020 --> 00:49:42,980
I'm an attorney. I'm obligated to...

843
00:49:43,100 --> 00:49:45,060
proceed according to the rule of law.

844
00:49:46,440 --> 00:49:47,560
Not agreed.

845
00:49:54,150 --> 00:49:56,200
This stinks, sisters.

846
00:49:56,280 --> 00:49:58,160
(CAT YOWLS)

847
00:49:59,660 --> 00:50:01,700
Did he just throw my cat out the window?

848
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:04,790
I don't think so.

849
00:50:04,870 --> 00:50:05,870
- No. Did he?
- Jopling?

850
00:50:10,920 --> 00:50:12,340
(FAINT SCRAPING)

851
00:50:16,590 --> 00:50:18,140
(DOOR OPENS IN THE DISTANCE)

852
00:50:18,260 --> 00:50:19,260
Shh-shh.

853
00:50:28,270 --> 00:50:31,150
(GUARD SNEEZES AND WALKS OFF)

854
00:50:32,860 --> 00:50:33,660
LUDWIG: Okay.

855
00:50:45,410 --> 00:50:47,360
There's something I haven't told you, Agatha.

856
00:50:48,380 --> 00:50:49,780
- Okay.
- We stole a painting.

857
00:50:49,790 --> 00:50:52,540
It's very valuable, maybe five million Klubecks, in fact.

858
00:50:52,550 --> 00:50:54,560
I don't know if anyone's even noticed it's missing yet,

859
00:50:54,630 --> 00:50:56,400
but if something should happen to me...

860
00:50:56,420 --> 00:50:57,930
You steal art?

861
00:50:58,680 --> 00:51:02,060
One picture. Anyway, we need to make a plan for your survival.

862
00:51:02,260 --> 00:51:05,260
Hide this. It's in code and you might need a magnifying glass to read it,

863
00:51:05,270 --> 00:51:07,520
but it tells you exactly where and how to find <i>Boy with Apple.</i>

864
00:51:07,630 --> 00:51:09,970
Don't take less than half the retail asking price. Also...

865
00:51:09,980 --> 00:51:11,380
Zero, I'm a baker.

866
00:51:11,400 --> 00:51:12,690
You're a pastry chef.

867
00:51:12,900 --> 00:51:14,780
I'm not a fence, if that's the term.

868
00:51:14,980 --> 00:51:16,860
I don't trade in stolen property.

869
00:51:18,110 --> 00:51:20,020
I said it wrong. She willed it to him.

870
00:51:20,030 --> 00:51:21,410
(DOOR OPENING NEARBY)

871
00:51:29,330 --> 00:51:30,330
Go to sleep.

872
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:33,060
Yes, <i>Herr</i> Mendl.

873
00:51:36,590 --> 00:51:37,660
- Hide this.
- No.

874
00:51:38,550 --> 00:51:39,880
Okay, but take it anyway.

875
00:53:16,810 --> 00:53:17,820
(DOOR OPENS)

876
00:53:31,620 --> 00:53:33,040
(DOOR OPENS)

877
00:53:39,500 --> 00:53:40,960
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

878
00:54:29,180 --> 00:54:30,890
(SCREAMING) (THUDDING)

879
00:55:06,550 --> 00:55:09,760
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>The next morning,</i> Herr <i>Becker received a peculiar,</i>

880
00:55:09,880 --> 00:55:12,680
<i>last-minute notice from the office of Deputy Kovacs,</i>

881
00:55:12,890 --> 00:55:17,350
<i>postponing their scheduled meeting, in perpetuity.</i>

882
00:55:41,330 --> 00:55:42,840
(METALLIC CLANG)

883
00:55:42,960 --> 00:55:44,300
(BELL RINGS)

884
00:55:47,470 --> 00:55:48,470
LUDWIG: Let's blow.

885
00:56:59,250 --> 00:57:00,500
How did you get out there?

886
00:57:00,620 --> 00:57:01,870
Shut the fuck up.

887
00:57:02,080 --> 00:57:04,710
These guys are trying to escape.

888
00:57:04,920 --> 00:57:06,630
What's wrong with you, you goddamn snitch?

889
00:57:06,840 --> 00:57:08,840
Guard! Guard! (CHOKING)

890
00:57:16,140 --> 00:57:17,390
It's you. Thank you.

891
00:57:17,470 --> 00:57:19,600
Thank you, you sweet, kind man.

892
00:57:49,460 --> 00:57:51,460
LUDWIG: Okay.

893
00:57:53,970 --> 00:57:55,430
(ALL GRUNTING SOFTLY)

894
00:58:43,520 --> 00:58:44,850
ALL: Whoa!

895
00:58:44,930 --> 00:58:46,480
(GUARDS SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY)

896
00:58:48,230 --> 00:58:49,610
(CRASHING)

897
00:58:49,770 --> 00:58:51,110
(GRUNTING)

898
00:58:55,780 --> 00:58:56,780
(GROANING)

899
00:58:56,950 --> 00:58:57,950
(STRAINING)

900
00:58:58,160 --> 00:58:59,620
(GROANS)

901
00:59:05,500 --> 00:59:08,120
I suppose you'd call that a draw.

902
00:59:10,460 --> 00:59:11,960
(DISTANT CLANGING)

903
00:59:17,260 --> 00:59:18,260
Good evening.

904
00:59:26,180 --> 00:59:27,440
(GRUNTING)

905
00:59:27,520 --> 00:59:31,150
Let me introduce you. Pinky, Wolf, Ludwig, this is the divine Zero.

906
00:59:31,360 --> 00:59:33,190
Günther was slain in the catacombs.

907
00:59:33,440 --> 00:59:36,190
Well, boys, who knows when we'll all meet again, but if one day...

908
00:59:36,440 --> 00:59:37,490
(CAR APPROACHES) Hold it.

909
00:59:40,860 --> 00:59:42,080
No time to gab.

910
00:59:43,120 --> 00:59:45,260
Take care of yourself, Mr. Gustave.

911
00:59:45,660 --> 00:59:46,660
Good luck, kid.

912
00:59:51,290 --> 00:59:52,790
Which way to the safe house?

913
00:59:52,920 --> 00:59:54,380
I couldn't find one.

914
00:59:54,420 --> 00:59:57,550
No safe house? Really? We're completely on our own out here?

915
00:59:57,720 --> 01:00:00,890
I'm afraid so. I asked around, but... (MUMBLES INDISTINCTLY)

916
01:00:01,140 --> 01:00:03,980
GUSTAVE H: I understand. Too risky. We'll just have to wing it, I suppose.

917
01:00:04,140 --> 01:00:06,520
Let's put on our disguises.

918
01:00:06,680 --> 01:00:07,730
We're wearing them.

919
01:00:07,850 --> 01:00:08,850
No, we're not.

920
01:00:09,060 --> 01:00:11,660
We said false whiskers and fake noses and so on.

921
01:00:11,690 --> 01:00:12,860
You didn't bring any?

922
01:00:13,020 --> 01:00:14,360
I thought you were growing one.

923
01:00:14,400 --> 01:00:17,480
And it wouldn't look realistic, would it?

924
01:00:17,530 --> 01:00:19,860
No, when done properly, they're perfectly convincing.

925
01:00:19,900 --> 01:00:21,860
But I take your point. So be it.

926
01:00:22,030 --> 01:00:24,660
Give me a few squirts of L'air de Panache, please, will you?

927
01:00:26,200 --> 01:00:27,830
Can I not get a squirt, even?

928
01:00:27,950 --> 01:00:29,540
I forgot the L'air de Panache.

929
01:00:29,750 --> 01:00:32,330
Honestly, you forgot the L'air de Panache?

930
01:00:32,540 --> 01:00:35,600
I don't believe it. How could you?

931
01:00:35,710 --> 01:00:38,670
I've been in jail, Zero! Do you understand how humiliating this is?

932
01:00:38,880 --> 01:00:40,880
I smell.

933
01:00:42,390 --> 01:00:44,760
(CHUCKLES) That's just marvelous, isn't it?

934
01:00:45,010 --> 01:00:47,220
I suppose this is to be expected back in...

935
01:00:47,310 --> 01:00:48,720
Where do you come from again?

936
01:00:48,930 --> 01:00:50,430
- Aq Salim al-Jabat.
- Precisely.

937
01:00:50,690 --> 01:00:53,230
I suppose this is to be expected back in Aq Salim al-Jabat...

938
01:00:53,400 --> 01:00:54,880
where one's prized possessions...

939
01:00:54,900 --> 01:00:56,930
are a stack of filthy carpets and a starving goat,

940
01:00:56,940 --> 01:01:00,940
and one sleeps behind a tent flap and survives on wild dates and scarabs.

941
01:01:00,950 --> 01:01:03,260
But it's not how I trained you.

942
01:01:03,700 --> 01:01:05,380
What on God's earth possessed you to leave...

943
01:01:05,410 --> 01:01:06,740
the homeland where you obviously belong...

944
01:01:06,910 --> 01:01:08,290
and travel unspeakable distances...

945
01:01:08,410 --> 01:01:12,200
to become a penniless immigrant in a refined, highly-cultivated society...

946
01:01:12,210 --> 01:01:15,360
that, quite frankly, could've gotten along very well without you?

947
01:01:16,130 --> 01:01:17,130
The war.

948
01:01:18,250 --> 01:01:19,590
Say again?

949
01:01:19,760 --> 01:01:21,800
Well, you see, my father was murdered...

950
01:01:22,050 --> 01:01:24,860
and the rest of my family were executed by firing squad.

951
01:01:24,890 --> 01:01:26,530
Our village was burned to the ground...

952
01:01:26,550 --> 01:01:29,660
and those who managed to survive were forced to flee.

953
01:01:29,930 --> 01:01:31,770
I left because of the war.

954
01:01:32,730 --> 01:01:36,440
I see. So you're, actually, really more of a refugee, in that sense?

955
01:01:37,860 --> 01:01:38,610
Truly.

956
01:01:38,770 --> 01:01:42,660
Well, I suppose I'd better take back everything I just said.

957
01:01:43,740 --> 01:01:46,980
What a bloody idiot I am. Pathetic fool.

958
01:01:47,070 --> 01:01:49,360
Goddamn, selfish bastard.

959
01:01:49,410 --> 01:01:52,460
This is disgraceful, and it's beneath the standards of the Grand Budapest.

960
01:01:54,120 --> 01:01:56,380
I apologize on behalf of the hotel.

961
01:01:56,420 --> 01:01:58,950
It's not your fault, Mr. Gustave. You were just upset I forgot the perfume.

962
01:01:58,960 --> 01:02:02,970
Don't make excuses for me. I owe you my life.

963
01:02:03,170 --> 01:02:07,010
You are my dear friend and protégé and I'm very proud of you.

964
01:02:07,260 --> 01:02:08,510
You must know that.

965
01:02:09,760 --> 01:02:11,770
I'm so sorry, Zero.

966
01:02:13,310 --> 01:02:14,360
We're brothers.

967
01:02:23,490 --> 01:02:25,490
How's our darling Agatha?

968
01:02:27,950 --> 01:02:31,400
"'Twas first light when I saw her face upon the heath,

969
01:02:31,450 --> 01:02:34,240
"and hence did I return, day-by-day, entranced,

970
01:02:34,290 --> 01:02:37,320
"tho' vinegar did brine my heart, never..." (ALARM BLARES)

971
01:02:37,330 --> 01:02:40,200
Very good. I'm going to stop you there because the alarm has sounded,

972
01:02:40,210 --> 01:02:43,060
but remember where we left off because I insist you finish later.

973
01:02:52,720 --> 01:02:55,060
(POLICE WHISTLE BLOWS)

974
01:03:01,690 --> 01:03:04,440
I want roadblocks at every junction for 50km.

975
01:03:04,650 --> 01:03:07,360
I want rail blocks at every train station for 100km.

976
01:03:07,610 --> 01:03:09,200
I want 50 men and 10 bloodhounds...

977
01:03:09,280 --> 01:03:10,450
ready in five minutes.

978
01:03:10,660 --> 01:03:12,200
We're going to strip-search...

979
01:03:12,330 --> 01:03:13,870
every pretzel<i>-Haus, Waffel-</i>hut, <i>Biergarten</i>

980
01:03:14,040 --> 01:03:16,120
and especially every grand hotel...

981
01:03:16,210 --> 01:03:17,830
from Äugenzburg to Zilchbrück.

982
01:03:18,120 --> 01:03:20,040
These men are dangerous, professional criminals.

983
01:03:20,290 --> 01:03:22,160
At least three of them are anyway.

984
01:03:22,550 --> 01:03:23,550
Who are you?

985
01:03:25,970 --> 01:03:26,970
What are you doing here?

986
01:03:27,050 --> 01:03:28,720
Civilian personnel aren't permitted in the cellblock.

987
01:03:28,800 --> 01:03:30,300
This is a military investigation.

988
01:03:30,430 --> 01:03:31,930
This is Mr. Jopling, sir.

989
01:03:32,010 --> 01:03:34,220
His employer's mother was one of the victims...

990
01:03:34,390 --> 01:03:35,160
Shut up.

991
01:03:37,730 --> 01:03:39,560
You work for the family Desgoffe und Taxis?

992
01:03:40,230 --> 01:03:43,860
Are you aware of the murder of Deputy Vilmos Kovacs on 23rd October?

993
01:03:44,400 --> 01:03:46,740
I'm aware of his disappearance.

994
01:03:46,990 --> 01:03:49,490
His body was found stuffed in a sarcophagus behind a storage room...

995
01:03:49,700 --> 01:03:51,240
at the Kunstmuseum late last night.

996
01:03:51,490 --> 01:03:54,080
<i>He was short four fingers.</i>

997
01:03:54,240 --> 01:03:55,540
What do you know about that?

998
01:03:55,660 --> 01:03:56,910
Nothing.

999
01:03:58,580 --> 01:04:00,580
Escort Mr. Jopling off the premises.

1000
01:04:11,090 --> 01:04:12,100
Mendl's.

1001
01:04:17,660 --> 01:04:20,260
Operator, get me the Excelsior Palace in Baden-Jürgen...

1002
01:04:20,270 --> 01:04:21,770
and reverse the charges, please.

1003
01:04:22,020 --> 01:04:23,860
We've no choice. There's nowhere else to turn.

1004
01:04:24,060 --> 01:04:25,360
I'll hold. Thank you.

1005
01:04:25,420 --> 01:04:28,280
It's our only hope. Otherwise, I shouldn't even mention its existence to you.

1006
01:04:28,440 --> 01:04:31,440
It goes without saying, never breathe a word about this to a living soul.

1007
01:04:31,450 --> 01:04:33,200
- Do you swear?
- Of course. What is it, in fact?

1008
01:04:33,410 --> 01:04:34,160
I can't say.

1009
01:04:37,200 --> 01:04:39,180
How does one come by front-row aisle seats...

1010
01:04:39,200 --> 01:04:42,460
for a first night at the Opera Toscana with one day's notice?

1011
01:04:42,630 --> 01:04:44,360
How does one arrange a private viewing...

1012
01:04:44,380 --> 01:04:47,130
of the tapestry collection at the Royal Saxon Gallery?

1013
01:04:47,300 --> 01:04:51,470
How does one secure a corner table at Chez Dominique on a Thursday?

1014
01:04:51,630 --> 01:04:53,930
Ivan, darling, it's Gustave. Hello.

1015
01:04:54,140 --> 01:04:56,140
Well, I was until about five minutes ago.

1016
01:04:56,390 --> 01:04:59,480
We've taken it upon ourselves to clear out in a hurry if you see what I mean.

1017
01:04:59,640 --> 01:05:02,300
Well, through a sewer, as it happens. Exactly.

1018
01:05:02,310 --> 01:05:04,980
Listen, Ivan, I'm sorry to cut you off, but we're in a bit of a bind.

1019
01:05:05,150 --> 01:05:06,820
This is an official request.

1020
01:05:06,980 --> 01:05:10,150
I'm formally calling upon the special services of...

1021
01:05:15,410 --> 01:05:16,560
IVAN: <i>I'll call you back, Gustave.</i>

1022
01:05:17,080 --> 01:05:18,660
Right. Stand by.

1023
01:05:19,240 --> 01:05:22,000
I beg your pardon. Do you prefer to walk?

1024
01:05:22,620 --> 01:05:24,500
We're right here. It's very simple.

1025
01:05:24,750 --> 01:05:26,180
Straight down the corniche, then left.

1026
01:05:26,960 --> 01:05:28,260
Jojo, see the gentleman out.

1027
01:05:31,840 --> 01:05:34,130
Get me <i>Monsieur</i> Georges at the Château Luxe, please.

1028
01:05:34,220 --> 01:05:35,720
(RINGING)

1029
01:05:35,930 --> 01:05:38,310
(SINGING) <i>Happy birthday to you</i>

1030
01:05:38,390 --> 01:05:39,520
(WHISPERS INDISTINCTLY)

1031
01:05:39,600 --> 01:05:40,600
Take over.

1032
01:05:40,680 --> 01:05:42,260
<i>Happy birthday, dear...</i>

1033
01:05:42,270 --> 01:05:43,220
GEORGES: <i>Hello, Ivan?</i>

1034
01:05:45,690 --> 01:05:46,520
Got it.

1035
01:05:47,770 --> 01:05:50,530
Get me <i>Monsieur</i> Dino at the Palazzo Principessa, please.

1036
01:05:50,610 --> 01:05:52,070
(RINGING)

1037
01:05:52,280 --> 01:05:54,610
Higher, goddammit, higher!

1038
01:05:54,700 --> 01:05:56,120
(SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

1039
01:05:56,530 --> 01:05:57,400
Take over.

1040
01:05:58,370 --> 01:05:59,950
- DINO: Monsieur <i>Georges.</i>
- Higher.

1041
01:06:00,560 --> 01:06:02,330
I see. Straightaway.

1042
01:06:03,870 --> 01:06:06,380
Get me <i>Monsieur</i> Robin at l'Hôtel Côte du Cap, please.

1043
01:06:06,460 --> 01:06:07,960
(RINGING)

1044
01:06:08,130 --> 01:06:10,000
...and two and three.

1045
01:06:11,300 --> 01:06:14,130
<i>Monsieur</i> Robin. There's a call from Monsieur Dino for you.

1046
01:06:14,340 --> 01:06:15,260
Take over.

1047
01:06:17,720 --> 01:06:19,050
One, two, three.

1048
01:06:19,180 --> 01:06:20,470
ROBIN: <i>Yes, Dino.</i>

1049
01:06:20,880 --> 01:06:23,980
Yes, Dino. Okay, Dino.

1050
01:06:25,730 --> 01:06:28,310
Get me <i>Monsieur</i> Martin at the Ritz Imperial, please.

1051
01:06:28,400 --> 01:06:29,900
(RINGING)

1052
01:06:30,020 --> 01:06:31,000
Too much salt.

1053
01:06:32,070 --> 01:06:33,010
Not enough pepper.

1054
01:06:33,690 --> 01:06:35,030
(WHISPERS)

1055
01:06:35,200 --> 01:06:36,200
Take over.

1056
01:06:39,240 --> 01:06:41,330
- <i>Robin? Martin.</i>
- Too much salt.

1057
01:06:41,540 --> 01:06:42,540
So I've heard.

1058
01:06:44,870 --> 01:06:47,830
Maybe. Let me make a few calls.

1059
01:06:50,880 --> 01:06:52,250
(VOCALIZING IMPATIENTLY)

1060
01:06:52,920 --> 01:06:55,420
Serge X, missing. Deputy Kovacs, also missing.

1061
01:06:55,590 --> 01:06:58,430
Madame D, dead. <i>Boy with Apple,</i> stolen, by us.

1062
01:06:58,590 --> 01:07:01,060
Dmitri and Jopling, ruthless, cold-blooded savages.

1063
01:07:01,260 --> 01:07:04,520
Gustave H, at large. What else?

1064
01:07:04,730 --> 01:07:06,020
Zero, confused.

1065
01:07:06,140 --> 01:07:07,440
Zero, confused, indeed.

1066
01:07:07,600 --> 01:07:09,770
The plot "thickens," as they say.

1067
01:07:10,020 --> 01:07:12,610
Why, by the way? Is it a soup metaphor?

1068
01:07:12,860 --> 01:07:14,860
I don't know. (CAR HONKS)

1069
01:07:19,700 --> 01:07:21,280
Get in.

1070
01:07:23,740 --> 01:07:24,880
IVAN: We found the butler.

1071
01:07:25,250 --> 01:07:28,040
He's hiding out in the remote foothills near Gabelmeister's Peak.

1072
01:07:28,250 --> 01:07:30,380
Our contact convinced him to meet you midday tomorrow...

1073
01:07:30,580 --> 01:07:31,960
at the observatory on the summit.

1074
01:07:32,130 --> 01:07:34,460
Tell no one. He'll explain everything.

1075
01:07:34,710 --> 01:07:36,760
Your train departs in four and a half minutes.

1076
01:07:36,970 --> 01:07:38,380
Here's your tickets.

1077
01:07:38,590 --> 01:07:39,640
Third class?

1078
01:07:39,800 --> 01:07:40,800
It was overbooked

1079
01:07:41,050 --> 01:07:44,640
but the conductor used to be a sommelier at the old Versailles.

1080
01:07:44,810 --> 01:07:45,970
He pulled some strings.

1081
01:07:46,270 --> 01:07:48,270
You'll need these for the dining car.

1082
01:07:51,150 --> 01:07:52,150
One last thing.

1083
01:07:54,660 --> 01:07:55,680
L'air de Panache.

1084
01:07:59,320 --> 01:08:01,490
They only had the half-ounce.

1085
01:08:05,790 --> 01:08:07,980
We should give him something as a symbolic gesture.

1086
01:08:08,000 --> 01:08:10,250
- How much money you got?
- 42 Klubecks and 3 postage stamps.

1087
01:08:10,460 --> 01:08:12,460
Give me 25. Yes.

1088
01:08:16,170 --> 01:08:17,150
Bless you.

1089
01:08:17,670 --> 01:08:19,090
Please.

1090
01:08:35,480 --> 01:08:36,570
JOPLING: I've got to hand it to him.

1091
01:08:36,650 --> 01:08:37,650
DMITRI ON PHONE: <i>Jesus Christ.</i>

1092
01:08:37,820 --> 01:08:41,570
JOPLING: I didn't see that coming. Well, what do you want next?

1093
01:08:41,700 --> 01:08:47,040
DMITRI: <i>Talk to the club-footed sister again, and, this time, be persuasive.</i>

1094
01:08:54,170 --> 01:08:56,960
DMITRI: Goddamn son of a bitch.

1095
01:09:20,530 --> 01:09:22,530
Holy fuck!

1096
01:09:26,870 --> 01:09:28,750
What's the meaning of this shit?

1097
01:09:29,910 --> 01:09:31,540
<i>Boy with Apple?</i> I thought you'd hidden it.

1098
01:09:31,620 --> 01:09:34,090
- Why are you only noticing now?
- I assumed it went to the tax appraiser.

1099
01:09:34,330 --> 01:09:36,000
Are you fucking kidding me?

1100
01:09:36,080 --> 01:09:37,090
CLOTILDE: <i>Excusez-moi.</i>

1101
01:09:38,590 --> 01:09:41,380
I believe it was removed by <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

1102
01:09:49,260 --> 01:09:50,590
I'm not angry with Serge.

1103
01:09:50,600 --> 01:09:53,060
You can't blame someone for their basic lack of moral fiber.

1104
01:09:53,270 --> 01:09:55,230
He's a frightened, little, yellow-bellied coward.

1105
01:09:55,440 --> 01:09:57,440
- It's not his fault, is it?
- I don't know. It depends.

1106
01:09:57,690 --> 01:09:59,230
Well, you can say that about most anything.

1107
01:09:59,320 --> 01:10:00,730
"It depends." Of course it depends.

1108
01:10:00,940 --> 01:10:02,440
Of course it depends. Of course it depends.

1109
01:10:02,690 --> 01:10:04,780
Yes, I suppose you're right. Of course it depends.

1110
01:10:05,030 --> 01:10:08,450
That doesn't mean I'm not going to throttle the little swamp rat.

1111
01:10:10,950 --> 01:10:15,250
May I officiate, by the way? The ceremony?

1112
01:10:15,460 --> 01:10:16,460
With pleasure.

1113
01:10:16,630 --> 01:10:19,250
I must say, I find that girl utterly delightful.

1114
01:10:19,460 --> 01:10:20,960
Flat as a board, enormous birthmark...

1115
01:10:21,210 --> 01:10:22,970
the shape of Mexico over half her face,

1116
01:10:23,220 --> 01:10:25,460
sweating for hours on end in that sweltering kitchen...

1117
01:10:25,470 --> 01:10:26,890
while Mendl, genius though he is,

1118
01:10:27,090 --> 01:10:28,880
looms over her like a hulking gorilla.

1119
01:10:28,890 --> 01:10:31,060
Yet without question, without fail,

1120
01:10:31,270 --> 01:10:34,480
always and invariably, she's exceedingly lovely.

1121
01:10:34,640 --> 01:10:35,980
Why?

1122
01:10:36,230 --> 01:10:37,810
Because of her purity.

1123
01:10:40,900 --> 01:10:43,150
She admires you as well, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave.

1124
01:10:43,320 --> 01:10:44,650
Does she?

1125
01:10:44,780 --> 01:10:46,070
Very much.

1126
01:10:46,280 --> 01:10:49,560
That's a good sign, you know. It means she "gets it."

1127
01:10:50,660 --> 01:10:52,060
That's important.

1128
01:10:54,750 --> 01:10:55,960
Don't flirt with her.

1129
01:11:08,140 --> 01:11:09,760
(THUDDING)

1130
01:11:25,860 --> 01:11:27,320
(GLASS SHATTERING IN DISTANCE)

1131
01:11:34,620 --> 01:11:36,290
(LOCK CLICKING)

1132
01:11:42,630 --> 01:11:46,050
LIEUTENANT: A radio telegram was delivered and signed for by the girl at 4:00 AM.

1133
01:11:46,210 --> 01:11:48,880
The envelope was found near the body, but its contents were missing.

1134
01:11:48,960 --> 01:11:50,970
However, the telegraph office always keeps...

1135
01:11:51,050 --> 01:11:52,800
a carbon of the ticker tape for 24 hours.

1136
01:11:53,010 --> 01:11:55,220
I copied it down. It reads as follows,

1137
01:11:56,220 --> 01:11:57,480
"Pack your things stop.

1138
01:11:57,640 --> 01:11:59,730
"Be ready to leave at moment's notice stop.

1139
01:11:59,900 --> 01:12:02,360
"Hide-out is vicinity of Gabelmeister's Peak stop.

1140
01:12:02,560 --> 01:12:04,980
"Destroy this message all my love full stop."

1141
01:12:06,070 --> 01:12:07,860
- Where's the basket?
- (SNAPS FINGERS)

1142
01:12:33,180 --> 01:12:34,680
PUMP ATTENDANT: Where you headed, mister?

1143
01:12:38,890 --> 01:12:41,230
Skiing? Sledding? Mountain climbing?

1144
01:12:50,950 --> 01:12:51,980
Three Klubecks.

1145
01:13:00,040 --> 01:13:01,460
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING)

1146
01:13:03,040 --> 01:13:04,990
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>By express wireless, I wrote Agatha...</i>

1147
01:13:05,040 --> 01:13:07,800
<i>with instructions to move to our pre-arranged hideout,</i>

1148
01:13:07,960 --> 01:13:10,470
<i>a Gypsy caravan on the outer Nebelsbad road,</i>

1149
01:13:10,720 --> 01:13:12,660
<i>while</i> Monsieur <i>Gustave and I continued east...</i>

1150
01:13:12,720 --> 01:13:14,090
<i>into the Zubrowkian Alps,</i>

1151
01:13:14,300 --> 01:13:18,060
<i>toward our high-altitude rendezvous with the butler Serge X.</i>

1152
01:13:19,980 --> 01:13:22,270
<i>As a precaution, we disembarked quietly...</i>

1153
01:13:22,480 --> 01:13:24,900
<i>in the freight yard just outside the station.</i>

1154
01:13:28,900 --> 01:13:29,990
(SNIFFING)

1155
01:13:32,490 --> 01:13:34,320
L'air de Panache.

1156
01:13:44,960 --> 01:13:47,450
It's a hell of a view. I give them that, for what's it's worth.

1157
01:13:47,460 --> 01:13:48,460
I agree.

1158
01:14:00,520 --> 01:14:03,980
"'Tis oft'-remarked, no single, falling-flake does any other...

1159
01:14:04,230 --> 01:14:06,180
- "in its pure and perfect form..."
- Somebody's coming.

1160
01:14:10,360 --> 01:14:13,530
Are you <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave of the Grand Budapest Hotel in Nebelsbad?

1161
01:14:14,080 --> 01:14:14,780
Uh-huh.

1162
01:14:14,810 --> 01:14:16,360
Get on the next cable car.

1163
01:14:55,900 --> 01:14:59,320
Are you <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave of the Grand Budapest Hotel in Nebelsbad?

1164
01:14:59,530 --> 01:15:00,530
Uh-huh.

1165
01:15:00,660 --> 01:15:01,660
Switch with me.

1166
01:15:28,140 --> 01:15:29,400
(UNLOCKING)

1167
01:15:31,230 --> 01:15:34,160
Are you <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave of the Grand Budapest Hotel in Nebelsbad?

1168
01:15:34,190 --> 01:15:34,980
Uh-huh.

1169
01:15:35,650 --> 01:15:36,950
Put these on and sing.

1170
01:15:38,700 --> 01:15:40,120
(REVERENTLY CHANTING)

1171
01:16:02,470 --> 01:16:03,180
MONK: Psst.

1172
01:16:05,100 --> 01:16:06,380
Are you <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave of the...

1173
01:16:06,390 --> 01:16:07,560
Yes, dammit.

1174
01:16:07,770 --> 01:16:08,770
Confess.

1175
01:16:08,980 --> 01:16:10,770
I'm innocent.

1176
01:16:10,980 --> 01:16:12,810
Hmm? No, no.

1177
01:16:19,070 --> 01:16:20,320
(DOOR CLOSES)

1178
01:16:22,320 --> 01:16:25,090
Forgive me, <i>Monsieur</i> Gustave. I never meant to betray you.

1179
01:16:25,160 --> 01:16:26,580
They threatened my life and now...

1180
01:16:26,600 --> 01:16:28,120
they've murdered my only family.

1181
01:16:28,330 --> 01:16:29,910
No. Who did they kill this time?

1182
01:16:30,120 --> 01:16:31,250
My dear sister.

1183
01:16:31,370 --> 01:16:32,500
The girl with the club foot?

1184
01:16:32,670 --> 01:16:34,090
- Yes.
- Those fuckers!

1185
01:16:34,290 --> 01:16:35,900
I tried to warn you at the beginning.

1186
01:16:35,920 --> 01:16:37,480
I know, darling. Let's put that behind us.

1187
01:16:37,510 --> 01:16:39,010
Listen, I hate to put you on the spot,

1188
01:16:39,130 --> 01:16:40,680
but I really must ask you to clear my name.

1189
01:16:40,800 --> 01:16:43,850
- Obviously you're grieving... OK.
- There's more.

1190
01:16:44,100 --> 01:16:45,430
- To the story.
- I get it. Go on.

1191
01:16:45,640 --> 01:16:47,640
I was the official witness in Madame D's presence...

1192
01:16:47,850 --> 01:16:49,430
to the creation of a second will...

1193
01:16:49,640 --> 01:16:52,780
to be executed only in the event of her death by murder.

1194
01:16:52,850 --> 01:16:54,480
- A second will?
- Right.

1195
01:16:54,500 --> 01:16:56,150
- In case she got bumped off?
- Right.

1196
01:16:56,360 --> 01:16:57,780
- Uh-huh?
- But they destroyed it.

1197
01:16:57,980 --> 01:16:59,440
- Oh, dear.
- However...

1198
01:16:59,650 --> 01:17:01,150
- Uh-huh?
- I pulled a copy.

1199
01:17:01,360 --> 01:17:03,490
A second copy of the second will?

1200
01:17:03,700 --> 01:17:04,990
- Right.
- Uh-huh?

1201
01:17:07,700 --> 01:17:10,040
What does it say? Where is it? What's it all about, dammit?

1202
01:17:10,210 --> 01:17:11,210
Don't keep us in suspense.

1203
01:17:11,290 --> 01:17:13,000
This has been a complete fucking nightmare.

1204
01:17:13,210 --> 01:17:15,630
Just tell us what the fuck is going on!

1205
01:17:17,460 --> 01:17:18,880
Serge?

1206
01:17:19,010 --> 01:17:20,670
Serge? Serge! (CLATTERING)

1207
01:17:24,720 --> 01:17:27,060
Bloody hell. They've strangled the poor slob.

1208
01:17:55,040 --> 01:17:56,040
Come on! Let's go!

1209
01:18:04,010 --> 01:18:05,250
ZERO: What do we do if we catch him?

1210
01:18:05,260 --> 01:18:06,220
GUSTAVE H: I don't know.

1211
01:18:06,260 --> 01:18:08,720
He's a homicidal psychopath. Let's stop.

1212
01:18:08,930 --> 01:18:11,180
I can't. I can barely steer.

1213
01:19:19,000 --> 01:19:22,630
You sick, pathetic creep. I hate you.

1214
01:19:35,350 --> 01:19:37,770
"'If this do be me end, farewell!'

1215
01:19:37,850 --> 01:19:39,190
"cried the wounded piper-boy,

1216
01:19:40,770 --> 01:19:41,900
"whilst the muskets cracked...

1217
01:19:41,980 --> 01:19:43,360
"and the yeomen roared 'Hurrah!'

1218
01:19:43,520 --> 01:19:44,690
"And the ramparts fell.

1219
01:19:46,360 --> 01:19:49,950
"'Methinks me breathes me last, me fears!' said he..."

1220
01:19:50,160 --> 01:19:52,030
Holy shit! You got him! (SCREAMING)

1221
01:19:52,280 --> 01:19:54,200
Well done, Zero!

1222
01:19:58,920 --> 01:20:00,380
(POLICE WHISTLE BLOWING)

1223
01:20:05,380 --> 01:20:07,720
<i>Gustave H! You are a fugitive from justice!</i>

1224
01:20:07,970 --> 01:20:10,780
<i>Surrender lawfully and I will personally vouchsafe...</i>

1225
01:20:10,840 --> 01:20:11,840
<i>your fair treatment.</i>

1226
01:20:11,970 --> 01:20:13,660
<i>Do not attempt to flee!</i>

1227
01:20:13,720 --> 01:20:15,220
- ZERO: What do you think?
- I don't know.

1228
01:20:15,390 --> 01:20:16,810
I'd rather jump off this cliff right now...

1229
01:20:16,930 --> 01:20:18,010
than go back to fucking prison.

1230
01:20:18,520 --> 01:20:20,900
I say we steal that sick maniac's motorcycle, go fetch Agatha,

1231
01:20:21,150 --> 01:20:22,190
take back <i>Boy with Apple</i>...

1232
01:20:22,270 --> 01:20:24,120
and head for the Maltese Riviera, once and for all.

1233
01:20:24,150 --> 01:20:26,900
Very good! You're so extraordinary, Zero. Thank you.

1234
01:20:27,070 --> 01:20:29,800
A moment of silence in memoriam of a devoted servant...

1235
01:20:29,820 --> 01:20:32,530
killed violently during the conduct of his duties.

1236
01:20:35,490 --> 01:20:36,500
Goodbye, Serge.

1237
01:20:39,000 --> 01:20:41,000
Okay, let's go. (GUNSHOT FIRING)

1238
01:20:53,800 --> 01:20:55,600
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>The war began at midnight.</i>

1239
01:20:55,760 --> 01:20:58,270
<i>Pffeifelstad fell by lunch under heavy shelling,</i>

1240
01:20:58,470 --> 01:21:01,810
<i>and 10 battalions surged across the long western border.</i>

1241
01:21:02,020 --> 01:21:03,810
<i>High-command advanced to Nebelsbad.</i>

1242
01:21:14,370 --> 01:21:15,780
(AGATHA CLEARS THROAT)

1243
01:21:16,030 --> 01:21:19,660
Compliments of <i>Herr</i> Mendl, for the executive staff.

1244
01:21:22,790 --> 01:21:25,960
CHUCK: General Stieglitz requests a garden-view sitting room with an extra roll-away.

1245
01:21:26,170 --> 01:21:28,130
Let's put him in the Duke Leopold Suite.

1246
01:21:28,340 --> 01:21:30,010
Secretary Woroniecki's office cabled.

1247
01:21:30,210 --> 01:21:33,800
He's checking in a day early. Rooms 401-2-3.

1248
01:21:33,970 --> 01:21:35,640
Tell Tactical Logistics we're moving them...

1249
01:21:35,800 --> 01:21:37,350
to a standard double on the third floor.

1250
01:21:37,560 --> 01:21:39,390
They'll need more space than that.

1251
01:22:02,500 --> 01:22:07,170
GUSTAVE H: The beginning of the end of the end of the beginning has begun.

1252
01:22:07,340 --> 01:22:10,380
A sad finale played off-key on a broken-down saloon piano...

1253
01:22:10,590 --> 01:22:13,170
in the outskirts of a forgotten ghost town.

1254
01:22:13,340 --> 01:22:15,340
I'd rather not bear witness to such blasphemy.

1255
01:22:15,550 --> 01:22:16,550
Me neither.

1256
01:22:16,760 --> 01:22:19,060
The Grand Budapest has become a troops' barracks.

1257
01:22:19,260 --> 01:22:21,720
I shall never cross its threshold again in my lifetime.

1258
01:22:21,930 --> 01:22:23,430
- Me neither.
- Never again shall I...

1259
01:22:23,680 --> 01:22:26,690
Actually, I think we might be going in there right now after all.

1260
01:22:28,440 --> 01:22:29,940
Dmitri.

1261
01:22:32,900 --> 01:22:34,610
Agatha.

1262
01:22:44,880 --> 01:22:47,420
Good evening, Mr. Desgoffe und Taxis. I'm <i>Monsieur</i> Chuck.

1263
01:22:47,630 --> 01:22:50,880
We've booked you and your sisters into the King Ferdinand Suite.

1264
01:22:51,090 --> 01:22:53,050
Good evening. General von Shrecker asked me...

1265
01:22:53,260 --> 01:22:54,060
Who's that?

1266
01:22:54,720 --> 01:22:56,050
I beg your pardon?

1267
01:22:56,220 --> 01:22:58,970
I think that girl's got my picture. Excuse me.

1268
01:23:14,400 --> 01:23:15,450
(ELEVATOR GATE OPENS)

1269
01:23:15,570 --> 01:23:17,570
- AGATHA: Six.
- DMITRI: Hold it.

1270
01:23:20,740 --> 01:23:21,780
DMITRI: Six.

1271
01:23:24,950 --> 01:23:26,750
Compliments of <i>Herr</i> Mendl.

1272
01:23:42,930 --> 01:23:43,930
GUSTAVE H: Excuse me.

1273
01:23:44,100 --> 01:23:46,420
Have you seen a pastry girl with a package under her arm...

1274
01:23:46,430 --> 01:23:47,520
in the last minute and a half?

1275
01:23:47,770 --> 01:23:50,100
Yep. She just got on the elevator with Mr. Desgoffe und Taxis.

1276
01:23:50,270 --> 01:23:52,480
Thank you.

1277
01:23:52,690 --> 01:23:54,110
I'm sorry. Who are you?

1278
01:23:54,280 --> 01:23:55,900
Otto, sir, the new Lobby Boy.

1279
01:23:56,030 --> 01:23:57,610
You haven't been trained properly.

1280
01:23:57,820 --> 01:23:59,950
A Lobby Boy never provides information of that kind.

1281
01:24:00,110 --> 01:24:02,120
You're a stone wall. Understood?

1282
01:24:18,970 --> 01:24:20,060
Pretty picture.

1283
01:24:21,390 --> 01:24:22,390
Sixth floor.

1284
01:25:06,180 --> 01:25:07,890
Where's <i>Boy with Apple?</i>

1285
01:25:08,850 --> 01:25:11,100
None of your goddamn business!

1286
01:25:11,890 --> 01:25:15,360
I'm going to blast your candy-ass once and for all right now.

1287
01:25:25,030 --> 01:25:26,580
Drop your weapon!

1288
01:25:33,580 --> 01:25:35,380
(GUNSHOTS FIRING) (GRUNTING)

1289
01:25:48,810 --> 01:25:50,920
- Cease fire! Cease fire!
- Whoa!

1290
01:25:50,930 --> 01:25:51,880
Stop it!

1291
01:25:52,480 --> 01:25:54,060
Who's shooting who?

1292
01:25:54,230 --> 01:25:56,730
That's Gustave H! The escaped murderer and art thief.

1293
01:25:56,940 --> 01:25:58,320
I've got him cornered!

1294
01:25:58,320 --> 01:25:59,730
That's Dmitri Desgoffe und Taxis!

1295
01:25:59,940 --> 01:26:01,940
He's responsible for the killing of Deputy Kovacs,

1296
01:26:01,960 --> 01:26:04,280
Serge X and his club-footed sister, plus his own mother!

1297
01:26:06,570 --> 01:26:08,740
Nobody move. Everybody's under arrest.

1298
01:26:08,830 --> 01:26:10,040
(GLASS BREAKS) (WOMAN SCREAMS)

1299
01:26:10,120 --> 01:26:12,410
Who's out the window?

1300
01:26:12,580 --> 01:26:14,580
Agatha! (GUNFIRE CONTINUES)

1301
01:26:20,760 --> 01:26:21,800
310-bis.

1302
01:26:22,010 --> 01:26:23,220
Hang on! Here I come!

1303
01:26:23,300 --> 01:26:24,930
(HEAVY GUNFIRE)

1304
01:26:42,280 --> 01:26:43,780
(GRUNTS)

1305
01:26:48,370 --> 01:26:49,700
AGATHA: Something's on the back of the...

1306
01:26:49,780 --> 01:26:50,790
(BOTH SCREAM)

1307
01:26:50,870 --> 01:26:52,330
(THUDDING)

1308
01:26:56,120 --> 01:26:57,120
Agatha!

1309
01:26:57,290 --> 01:26:59,000
Agatha!

1310
01:26:59,210 --> 01:27:01,210
- Are you all right?
- I think so.

1311
01:27:02,880 --> 01:27:04,260
Something's on the back of the picture.

1312
01:27:08,720 --> 01:27:10,470
(PEOPLE MURMURING)

1313
01:27:18,230 --> 01:27:19,230
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>She left everything...</i>

1314
01:27:19,310 --> 01:27:21,020
<i>to</i> Monsieur <i>Gustave, of course.</i>

1315
01:27:22,980 --> 01:27:25,320
<i>The mansion, known as Schloss Lutz,</i>

1316
01:27:26,820 --> 01:27:29,910
<i>the factories, which produced weapons, medicine and textiles,</i>

1317
01:27:30,160 --> 01:27:31,910
(ALL LAUGHING)

1318
01:27:32,870 --> 01:27:34,540
<i>an important newspaper syndicate,</i>

1319
01:27:35,660 --> 01:27:38,040
<i>and perhaps you've already deduced...</i>

1320
01:27:38,370 --> 01:27:44,050
<i>this very institution, the Grand Budapest Hotel.</i>

1321
01:27:44,380 --> 01:27:45,880
<i>He anointed me his successor,</i>

1322
01:27:46,090 --> 01:27:49,010
<i>and as the war continued, I served my adopted country...</i>

1323
01:27:49,180 --> 01:27:52,390
<i>from the narrow desk still found against the wall in the next room.</i>

1324
01:27:52,510 --> 01:27:53,510
(INDISTINCT)

1325
01:27:53,850 --> 01:27:55,930
<i>He was the same as his disciples,</i>

1326
01:27:56,180 --> 01:27:59,520
<i>insecure, vain, superficial, blond, needy.</i>

1327
01:28:00,350 --> 01:28:02,570
<i>In the end he was even rich.</i>

1328
01:28:03,940 --> 01:28:07,110
<i>He did not succeed, however, in growing old.</i>

1329
01:28:11,870 --> 01:28:13,740
"Dearly beloved, we are gathered together..."

1330
01:28:13,950 --> 01:28:15,790
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>Nor did my darling Agatha.</i>

1331
01:28:16,040 --> 01:28:18,290
<i>She and our infant son would be killed two years later...</i>

1332
01:28:18,540 --> 01:28:21,380
<i>by the Prussian grippe. An absurd little disease.</i>

1333
01:28:21,580 --> 01:28:23,660
<i>Today we treat it in a single week,</i>

1334
01:28:23,680 --> 01:28:26,800
<i>but, in those days many millions died.</i>

1335
01:28:30,090 --> 01:28:31,930
<i>On the 21st day of the occupation,</i>

1336
01:28:32,140 --> 01:28:34,890
<i>the morning the independent state of Zubrowka...</i>

1337
01:28:35,060 --> 01:28:36,220
<i>officially ceased to exist,</i>

1338
01:28:36,390 --> 01:28:38,390
<i>we traveled with</i> Monsieur <i>Gustave to Lutz.</i>

1339
01:28:38,560 --> 01:28:40,810
In answer to your earlier question, by the way,

1340
01:28:41,180 --> 01:28:42,500
of course.

1341
01:28:44,080 --> 01:28:47,570
Zero asked me about my humble beginnings in the hotel trade.

1342
01:28:47,740 --> 01:28:50,270
I was, perhaps, for a time, considered the best Lobby Boy...

1343
01:28:50,280 --> 01:28:52,660
we'd ever had at the Grand Budapest.

1344
01:28:52,910 --> 01:28:54,330
I think I can say that.

1345
01:28:54,580 --> 01:28:56,580
This one finally surpassed me.

1346
01:28:56,620 --> 01:28:59,280
Although, I must say, he had an exceptional teacher.

1347
01:28:59,750 --> 01:29:00,660
Truly.

1348
01:29:06,170 --> 01:29:09,130
"Whence came these two radiant, celestial brothers,

1349
01:29:09,340 --> 01:29:10,630
"united, for an instant,

1350
01:29:10,840 --> 01:29:13,980
"as they crossed the stratosphere of our starry window?

1351
01:29:15,300 --> 01:29:18,100
"One from the East and one from the West."

1352
01:29:18,360 --> 01:29:20,020
Very good.

1353
01:29:20,270 --> 01:29:22,000
Don't flirt with her.

1354
01:29:23,350 --> 01:29:24,520
(BRAKES SCREECHING)

1355
01:29:24,610 --> 01:29:26,860
Why are we stopping at a barley field again?

1356
01:29:35,660 --> 01:29:38,330
I find these black uniforms very drab.

1357
01:29:42,830 --> 01:29:44,120
Well, hello there, chaps.

1358
01:29:44,210 --> 01:29:45,790
We were just talking about you.

1359
01:29:46,040 --> 01:29:47,360
Documents, please.

1360
01:29:47,560 --> 01:29:49,980
With pleasure, as always.

1361
01:29:54,220 --> 01:29:56,210
You're the first of the official death squads...

1362
01:29:56,220 --> 01:29:58,470
to whom we've been formally introduced.

1363
01:29:58,640 --> 01:29:59,640
How do you do?

1364
01:30:01,980 --> 01:30:03,890
<i>Plus ça change,</i> am I right?

1365
01:30:08,360 --> 01:30:11,740
Uh, that's a migratory visa with stage three worker status, darling.

1366
01:30:11,990 --> 01:30:12,990
Read this.

1367
01:30:22,330 --> 01:30:23,660
SOLDIER: Come outside.

1368
01:30:23,830 --> 01:30:25,170
GUSTAVE H: Now, stay there.

1369
01:30:27,380 --> 01:30:29,670
I give you my word, if you lay a finger on this man,

1370
01:30:29,880 --> 01:30:32,050
I'll see you dishonorably discharged,

1371
01:30:32,260 --> 01:30:35,340
locked up in the stockade and hanged by sundown.

1372
01:30:36,580 --> 01:30:38,510
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>There are still faint glimmers of civilization...</i>

1373
01:30:38,680 --> 01:30:40,850
<i>left in this barbaric slaughterhouse...</i>

1374
01:30:40,970 --> 01:30:43,060
<i>that was once known as humanity.</i>

1375
01:30:45,310 --> 01:30:46,110
(SHRIEKS)

1376
01:30:46,200 --> 01:30:49,160
You filthy, goddamn, pock-marked, fascist assholes!

1377
01:30:50,860 --> 01:30:52,360
MR. MOUSTAFA: <i>He was one of them.</i>

1378
01:30:53,700 --> 01:30:55,200
<i>What more is there to say?</i>

1379
01:31:03,410 --> 01:31:04,680
What happened in the end?

1380
01:31:06,250 --> 01:31:07,560
In the end they shot him.

1381
01:31:10,040 --> 01:31:11,260
So it all went to me.

1382
01:31:20,890 --> 01:31:24,140
YOUNG WRITER: <i>After dinner, we went to collect the keys to our rooms,</i>

1383
01:31:24,320 --> 01:31:26,360
<i>but</i> Monsieur <i>Jean had abandoned his post.</i>

1384
01:31:26,390 --> 01:31:28,560
I expect he's forgotten all about us.

1385
01:31:28,770 --> 01:31:29,900
YOUNG WRITER: <i>In recent years, of course,</i>

1386
01:31:30,060 --> 01:31:32,480
<i>such properties and holdings as the Grand Budapest...</i>

1387
01:31:32,570 --> 01:31:36,260
<i>had with very few exceptions become common property.</i>

1388
01:31:37,110 --> 01:31:39,360
<i>While the precise terms of his negotiation...</i>

1389
01:31:39,410 --> 01:31:41,280
<i>with the new government had never been announced,</i>

1390
01:31:41,490 --> 01:31:42,860
<i>the result was an open secret,</i>

1391
01:31:43,580 --> 01:31:46,460
<i>Zero Moustafa had traded a great and important fortune...</i>

1392
01:31:46,500 --> 01:31:50,790
<i>in exchange for one costly, unprofitable, doomed hotel.</i>

1393
01:31:51,840 --> 01:31:52,840
<i>Why?</i>

1394
01:31:53,110 --> 01:31:55,320
<i>Was it merely sentimental?</i>

1395
01:31:55,720 --> 01:31:59,090
<i>It was quite forward of me and a bit out of character,</i>

1396
01:31:59,260 --> 01:32:00,800
<i>but I felt I must know,</i>

1397
01:32:01,350 --> 01:32:03,350
<i>for my health, I suppose.</i>

1398
01:32:05,930 --> 01:32:08,140
Forgive me for asking. I hope I haven't upset you.

1399
01:32:08,350 --> 01:32:09,660
No, of course not.

1400
01:32:11,440 --> 01:32:14,270
Is it simply your last connection to that vanished world,

1401
01:32:14,440 --> 01:32:16,680
his world, if you will?

1402
01:32:16,990 --> 01:32:17,980
His world?

1403
01:32:18,950 --> 01:32:21,080
No, I don't think so.

1404
01:32:22,120 --> 01:32:24,200
You see, we shared a vocation.

1405
01:32:24,450 --> 01:32:26,160
It wouldn't have been necessary.

1406
01:32:26,660 --> 01:32:30,960
No. The hotel I keep for Agatha.

1407
01:32:32,500 --> 01:32:33,560
We were happy here.

1408
01:32:34,360 --> 01:32:35,660
For a little while.

1409
01:32:36,800 --> 01:32:37,800
(ELEVATOR BELL DINGS)

1410
01:32:41,340 --> 01:32:43,820
To be frank, I think his world had vanished...

1411
01:32:43,850 --> 01:32:45,720
long before he ever entered it.

1412
01:32:45,970 --> 01:32:47,520
But, I will say,

1413
01:32:47,720 --> 01:32:51,230
he certainly sustained the illusion with a marvelous grace.

1414
01:32:52,810 --> 01:32:55,020
- Are you going up?
- No, I'll sit for a little while.

1415
01:32:55,900 --> 01:32:56,860
Good night.

1416
01:32:57,530 --> 01:32:58,820
(ELEVATOR BELL DINGS)

1417
01:33:02,410 --> 01:33:03,490
YOUNG WRITER: <i>The next week,</i>

1418
01:33:03,700 --> 01:33:05,910
<i>I sailed for a cure in South America...</i>

1419
01:33:06,160 --> 01:33:08,750
<i>and began a long, wandering journey abroad.</i>

1420
01:33:09,000 --> 01:33:11,160
<i>I did not return to Europe for many years.</i>

1421
01:33:16,880 --> 01:33:19,340
<i>It was an enchanting old ruin.</i>

1422
01:33:24,550 --> 01:33:26,850
AUTHOR: <i>But I never managed to see it again.</i>

